ML_PAGE("formulas", "<header class=\"page-header theme-exam\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">94 FAMILIES · 382 FORMULAS · RANKED</span><h2>Formula Sheet</h2><p>Every formula in Chapters 1 to 12, explicit and implicit, ranked by how likely it is to matter in a calculation test. Each card opens with the <b>base formula</b>; its rearrangements and special cases sit underneath at lower priority. Use Recall mode to hide the base formulas and test yourself.</p></header><div class=\"ff-controls\"><div class=\"ff-chips\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Filter by chapter\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip on\" data-ff-chip=\"all\">All chapters</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-ff-chip=\"c1\">Ch 1</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-ff-chip=\"c2\">Ch 2</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-ff-chip=\"c3\">Ch 3</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-ff-chip=\"c4a\">App. 4A</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-ff-chip=\"c4\">Ch 4</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-ff-chip=\"c5\">Ch 5</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-ff-chip=\"c6\">Ch 6</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-ff-chip=\"c7\">Ch 7</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-ff-chip=\"c8\">Ch 8</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-ff-chip=\"c9\">Ch 9</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-ff-chip=\"c10\">Ch 10</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-ff-chip=\"c11\">Ch 11</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-chip\" data-ff-chip=\"c12\">Ch 12</button></div><div class=\"ff-tools\"><input id=\"ff-search\" class=\"ff-search\" type=\"search\" placeholder=\"Search: beta, margin, annuity, spread...\" aria-label=\"Search formulas\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn ff-recall\" data-ff-recall aria-pressed=\"false\">Recall mode</button><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-action=\"print\">Print</button></div></div><p class=\"ff-empty\" hidden>No formulas match. Clear the search or pick another chapter.</p><details class=\"ff-index\"><summary>One-line index of all 94 families</summary><div class=\"scroll-x\"><table class=\"comparison-table ff-table\"><thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Family</th><th>Base formula</th><th>Chapter</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr data-ff-ch=\"c4a\"><td>1</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-1\">Time value of a lump sum</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$FV = PV\\,(1+r)^n$$</div></td><td>Appendix 4A</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c5 c8\"><td>2</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-2\">CAPM and the Security Market Line</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$R_i = R_f + \\beta_i\\,\\bigl[E(R_m) - R_f\\bigr]$$</div></td><td>Chapter 5, Chapter 8</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c5\"><td>3</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-3\">Two-asset portfolio risk</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\sigma_p = \\sqrt{w_A^2\\sigma_A^2 + w_B^2\\sigma_B^2 + 2\\,w_A w_B\\,\\sigma_A\\sigma_B\\,\\rho_{AB}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 5</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c4 c6 c10 c11 c12\"><td>4</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-4\">Holding-period return (any investment)</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$HPR = \\dfrac{\\text{income} + (P_1 - P_0)}{P_0}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 4, Chapter 6, Chapter 10, Chapter 11, Chapter 12</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c7\"><td>5</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-5\">DuPont breakdown of ROE</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$ROE = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{NI}{\\text{sales}}}_{\\text{net margin}} \\times \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\text{sales}}{\\text{assets}}}_{\\text{asset turnover}} \\times \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\text{assets}}{\\text{equity}}}_{\\text{equity multiplier}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 7</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c7\"><td>6</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-6\">Return on assets and return on equity</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$ROA = \\dfrac{\\text{net profit after taxes}}{\\text{total assets}},\\qquad ROE = \\dfrac{\\text{net profit after taxes}}{\\text{stockholders' equity}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 7</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c6 c7\"><td>7</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-7\">Earnings per share and the P/E ratio</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$EPS = \\dfrac{NI - \\text{preferred dividends}}{\\text{common shares}},\\qquad P/E = \\dfrac{\\text{market price per share}}{EPS}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 6, Chapter 7</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c8\"><td>8</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-8\">Constant-growth dividend model (Gordon)</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$V_0 = \\dfrac{D_1}{k - g},\\qquad D_1 = D_0\\,(1+g),\\qquad k &gt; g$$</div></td><td>Chapter 8</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c10\"><td>9</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-9\">Conversion value of a convertible</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$CV = N \\times P_s$$</div></td><td>Chapter 10</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c10\"><td>10</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-10\">Conversion premium</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Premium (RM)} = P_c - CV$$</div></td><td>Chapter 10</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c1 c3 c10 c11\"><td>11</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-11\">Bond price (annual coupons)</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$P_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{N}\\dfrac{C}{(1+y)^t} + \\dfrac{F}{(1+y)^N}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 1, Chapter 3, Chapter 10, Chapter 11</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c12\"><td>12</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-12\">Net asset value (NAV) per share</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$NAV = \\dfrac{\\text{total assets} - \\text{total liabilities}}{\\text{shares outstanding}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 12</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c2\"><td>13</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-13\">Margin</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Margin} = \\dfrac{V - D}{V} = \\dfrac{\\text{investor equity}}{\\text{market value}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 2</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c5\"><td>14</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-14\">Portfolio beta</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\beta_p = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i\\,\\beta_i$$</div></td><td>Chapter 5</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c5\"><td>15</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-15\">Portfolio expected return</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$E(R_p) = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i\\,E(R_i),\\qquad \\sum w_i = 1$$</div></td><td>Chapter 5</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c4 c11\"><td>16</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-16\">Required return</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$R_{\\text{required}} = R_{\\text{real}} + IP + RP = R_f + RP$$</div></td><td>Chapter 4, Chapter 11</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c2 c6 c10 c12\"><td>17</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-17\">Home-currency return (foreign shares, bonds, funds)</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$(1 + R_{\\text{home}}) = (1 + R_{\\text{asset}})\\,(1 + R_{FX})$$</div></td><td>Chapter 2, Chapter 6, Chapter 10, Chapter 12</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c6 c7\"><td>18</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-18\">Dividend yield</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Dividend yield} = \\dfrac{\\text{annual dividend per share}}{\\text{current share price}} = \\dfrac{D}{P}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 6, Chapter 7</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c6 c7\"><td>19</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-19\">Dividend payout ratio and dividends per share</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Payout ratio} = \\dfrac{\\text{dividend per share}}{EPS}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 6, Chapter 7</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c7\"><td>20</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-20\">Current ratio and net working capital</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Current ratio} = \\dfrac{\\text{current assets}}{\\text{current liabilities}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 7</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c7\"><td>21</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-21\">Receivables turnover and collection period</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Receivables turnover} = \\dfrac{\\text{annual sales}}{\\text{accounts receivable}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 7</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c7\"><td>22</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-22\">Net profit margin</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Net profit margin} = \\dfrac{\\text{net profit after taxes}}{\\text{total revenues}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 7</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c8\"><td>23</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-23\">Two-stage (variable-growth) dividend model</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$V_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{n}\\dfrac{D_t}{(1+k)^t} + \\dfrac{P_n}{(1+k)^n},\\qquad P_n = \\dfrac{D_{n+1}}{k - g_2}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 8</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c8\"><td>24</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-24\">Forecast chain: sales to EPS, dividend and future price</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$EPS_1 = \\dfrac{\\text{Sales}_0\\,(1+g_s) \\times \\text{net margin}}{\\text{common shares}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 8</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c8\"><td>25</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-25\">Value of dividends plus a future sale price</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$V_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{N}\\dfrac{D_t}{(1+k)^t} + \\dfrac{P_N}{(1+k)^N}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 8</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c9\"><td>26</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-26\">Advance-decline: net breadth and the A-D line</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Net breadth}_t = A_t - D_t$$</div></td><td>Chapter 9</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c9\"><td>27</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-27\">TRIN (Arms Index)</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{TRIN} = \\dfrac{A / D}{AV / DV}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 9</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c9\"><td>28</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-28\">Simple moving average</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$SMA_n = \\dfrac{P_t + P_{t-1} + \\cdots + P_{t-n+1}}{n}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 9</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c10\"><td>29</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-29\">Taxable-equivalent yield</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$TEY = \\dfrac{y_{\\text{exempt}}}{1 - t}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 10</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c10 c11\"><td>30</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-30\">Current yield</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Current yield} = \\dfrac{\\text{annual coupon}}{\\text{current market price}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 10, Chapter 11</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c10\"><td>31</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-31\">Annual coupon</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$C = \\text{coupon rate} \\times \\text{par}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 10</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c11\"><td>32</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-32\">Bond price (semiannual coupons)</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$P_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{2N}\\dfrac{C/2}{(1+y/2)^t} + \\dfrac{F}{(1+y/2)^{2N}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 11</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c11\"><td>33</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-33\">Yield to maturity (YTM)</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$P_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{N}\\dfrac{C}{(1+YTM)^t} + \\dfrac{F}{(1+YTM)^N}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 11</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c11\"><td>34</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-34\">Macaulay duration</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$D_{Mac} = \\sum_{t=1}^{N} t \\times \\dfrac{PV(CF_t)}{P_0}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 11</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c11\"><td>35</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-35\">Modified duration and price sensitivity</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$D_{mod} = \\dfrac{D_{Mac}}{1+y},\\quad \\dfrac{\\Delta P}{P} \\approx -D_{mod}\\,\\Delta y$$</div></td><td>Chapter 11</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c12\"><td>36</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-36\">Premium or discount to NAV</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{premium or discount} = \\dfrac{\\text{market price} - NAV}{NAV}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 12</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c12\"><td>37</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-37\">Front-end load</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{amount invested} = \\text{amount paid} \\times (1 - L)$$</div></td><td>Chapter 12</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c2\"><td>38</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-38\">Return on equity with margin</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Return} = \\dfrac{\\text{income} - \\text{interest} + V_1 - V_0}{E_0}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 2</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c4a\"><td>39</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-39\">Ordinary annuity</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$FV = PMT\\left[\\dfrac{(1+r)^n - 1}{r}\\right]$$</div></td><td>Appendix 4A</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c4a\"><td>40</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-40\">Value of a cash-flow stream</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$PV = \\sum_{t=1}^{n} \\dfrac{CF_t}{(1+r)^t}$$</div></td><td>Appendix 4A</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c4\"><td>41</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-41\">Yield, IRR and growth rate</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$r = \\left(\\dfrac{FV}{PV}\\right)^{1/n} - 1$$</div></td><td>Chapter 4</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c6\"><td>42</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-42\">Ownership percentage and dilution</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Ownership} = \\dfrac{\\text{shares you hold}}{\\text{shares outstanding}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 6</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c6\"><td>43</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-43\">Market capitalisation</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Market cap} = P \\times N$$</div></td><td>Chapter 6</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c7\"><td>44</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-44\">Inventory turnover</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Inventory turnover} = \\dfrac{\\text{annual sales}}{\\text{inventory}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 7</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c7\"><td>45</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-45\">Total asset turnover</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Total asset turnover} = \\dfrac{\\text{annual sales}}{\\text{total assets}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 7</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c7\"><td>46</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-46\">Debt-equity ratio</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Debt-equity} = \\dfrac{\\text{long-term debt}}{\\text{stockholders' equity}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 7</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c7\"><td>47</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-47\">Times interest earned</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Times interest earned} = \\dfrac{EBIT}{\\text{interest expense}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 7</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c6 c7\"><td>48</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-48\">Book value per share and price-to-book</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$BVPS = \\dfrac{\\text{common shareholders' equity}}{\\text{common shares outstanding}},\\qquad P/B = \\dfrac{\\text{market price per share}}{BVPS}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 6, Chapter 7</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c8\"><td>49</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-49\">Expected return against the required return</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$E(R) = \\dfrac{D_1 + (P_1 - P_0)}{P_0} \\quad\\text{vs}\\quad k$$</div></td><td>Chapter 8</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c9\"><td>50</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-50\">On-balance volume (OBV)</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$OBV_t = OBV_{t-1} + \\begin{cases} +V_t &amp; \\text{if } P_t &gt; P_{t-1} \\\\ -V_t &amp; \\text{if } P_t &lt; P_{t-1} \\\\ 0 &amp; \\text{if } P_t = P_{t-1} \\end{cases}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 9</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c9\"><td>51</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-51\">Mutual fund cash ratio (MFCR)</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{MFCR} = \\dfrac{\\text{mutual fund cash}}{\\text{assets under management}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 9</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c9\"><td>52</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-52\">Abnormal (excess) return</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$AR = R_{\\text{actual}} - E(R)$$</div></td><td>Chapter 9</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c10\"><td>53</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-53\">Convertible payback period</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Payback} = \\dfrac{\\text{conversion premium}}{C - N \\times D}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 10</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c12\"><td>54</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-54\">HPR with reinvested distributions</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$HPR = \\dfrac{N_1 P_1 - N_0 P_0}{N_0 P_0},\\qquad N_1 = N_0 + \\dfrac{N_0 D}{P_{\\text{reinvest}}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 12</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c8\"><td>55</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-55\">Zero-growth dividend model</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$V_0 = \\dfrac{D}{k}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 8</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c2\"><td>56</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-56\">Short sale</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Profit} = (P_{\\text{sell}} - P_{\\text{buy back}}) \\times N$$</div></td><td>Chapter 2</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c4\"><td>57</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-57\">Standard deviation and CV</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$s = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sum_{t=1}^{n} (R_t - \\bar{R})^2}{n - 1}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 4</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c1 c4\"><td>58</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-58\">Real return</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$R_{\\text{real}} \\approx R_{\\text{nominal}} - i$$</div></td><td>Chapter 1, Chapter 4</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c1\"><td>59</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-59\">After-tax return</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$R_{\\text{after-tax}} = R_{\\text{pre-tax}}\\,(1 - t)$$</div></td><td>Chapter 1</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c2\"><td>60</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-60\">Bid, ask and spread</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Spread} = \\text{Ask} - \\text{Bid},\\qquad \\text{Bid} &lt; \\text{Ask}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 2</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c6\"><td>61</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-61\">Stock split mechanics</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$N_{\\text{new}} = k \\times N_{\\text{old}},\\qquad P_{\\text{new}} \\approx \\dfrac{P_{\\text{old}}}{k}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 6</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c7\"><td>62</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-62\">PEG ratio</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$PEG = \\dfrac{P/E}{\\text{3- to 5-year EPS growth rate, in percent}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 7</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c8\"><td>63</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-63\">Relative valuation (price multiples)</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Implied price} = \\text{per-share measure} \\times \\text{chosen multiple}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 8</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c11\"><td>64</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-64\">Yield to call (YTC)</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$P_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{N_c}\\dfrac{C}{(1+YTC)^t} + \\dfrac{CP}{(1+YTC)^{N_c}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 11</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c12\"><td>65</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-65\">Back-end load (deferred sales charge)</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{amount received} = \\text{redemption value} \\times (1 - L_b)$$</div></td><td>Chapter 12</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c12\"><td>66</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-66\">Expense ratio and operating expenses</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{annual operating expenses} = \\text{expense ratio} \\times \\text{average net assets}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 12</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c12\"><td>67</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-67\">After-cost ending value (fee drag)</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$FV = \\text{amount paid}\\,(1 - L)\\,(1 + r_{\\text{net}})^{n}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 12</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c3 c6\"><td>68</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-68\">Net trading profit and costs</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Net profit} = N\\,(P_1 - P_0) - (C_{\\text{buy}} + C_{\\text{sell}})$$</div></td><td>Chapter 3, Chapter 6</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c3\"><td>69</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-69\">Order execution rules</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Buy limit fills if } P \\le L,\\qquad \\text{Sell limit fills if } P \\ge L$$</div></td><td>Chapter 3</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c3 c12\"><td>70</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-70\">Relative performance</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Relative performance} = R_{\\text{portfolio}} - R_{\\text{benchmark}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 3, Chapter 12</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c6\"><td>71</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-71\">Dividend reinvestment and stock dividends</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Shares bought by a DRIP} = \\dfrac{n \\times D}{P_{\\text{reinvest}}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 6</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c7\"><td>72</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-72\">Accounting equation and net income</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Assets} = \\text{Liabilities} + \\text{Equity}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 7</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c7\"><td>73</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-73\">Growth compared with sales growth</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$g = \\dfrac{X_1 - X_0}{X_0}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 7</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c9\"><td>74</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-74\">Earnings surprise</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Surprise} = \\text{actual} - \\text{expected}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 9</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c10\"><td>75</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-75\">Call price</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Call price} = \\text{par} + \\text{call premium}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 10</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c10\"><td>76</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-76\">Zero-coupon bond</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$P_0 = \\dfrac{F}{(1 + r)^n}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 10</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c10 c11\"><td>77</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-77\">Yield spread and basis points</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Spread} = y_{\\text{bond}} - y_{\\text{benchmark}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 10, Chapter 11</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c2\"><td>78</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-78\">Long purchase</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Profit} = N\\,(P_1 - P_0) + \\text{dividends},\\qquad R = \\dfrac{P_1 - P_0}{P_0}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 2</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c6\"><td>79</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-79\">Decline from a market peak</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Decline} = \\dfrac{\\text{peak} - \\text{current level}}{\\text{peak}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 6</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c8\"><td>80</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-80\">General dividend valuation model</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$V_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{E(D_t)}{(1+k)^t}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 8</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c9\"><td>81</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-81\">New highs minus new lows</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$NH_t - NL_t$$</div></td><td>Chapter 9</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c11\"><td>82</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-82\">Immunization (duration matching)</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$PV_{\\text{assets}} = PV_{\\text{liability}},\\qquad D_{\\text{assets}} = D_{\\text{liability}}\\ (= H \\text{ for one payment at } H)$$</div></td><td>Chapter 11</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c3\"><td>83</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-83\">Index construction</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Price-weighted change} = \\dfrac{\\sum P_1}{\\sum P_0} - 1$$</div></td><td>Chapter 3</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c2\"><td>84</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-84\">Underwriting spread</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Spread} = P_{\\text{offer}} - P_{\\text{paid to issuer}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 2</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c6\"><td>85</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-85\">Residual claim in a liquidation</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Residual for common owners} = \\max(0,\\ \\text{amount available} - \\text{senior claims})$$</div></td><td>Chapter 6</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c7\"><td>86</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-86\">Cash-flow statement identities</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\Delta\\text{Cash} = CFO + CFI + CFF$$</div></td><td>Chapter 7</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c7\"><td>87</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-87\">Intrinsic value versus market price</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Upside to the estimate} = \\dfrac{V - P}{P}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 7</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c9\"><td>88</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-88\">Confidence index</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$CI = \\dfrac{\\text{yield on high-grade corporate bonds}}{\\text{yield on intermediate-grade corporate bonds}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 9</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c12\"><td>89</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-89\">Exchange trading cost of an ETF purchase</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{cost per purchase} \\approx \\text{commission} + \\text{spread or premium paid} \\times \\text{amount}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 12</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c1\"><td>90</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-90\">Liquidity discount</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Discount} = \\dfrac{\\text{fair value} - \\text{quick-sale price}}{\\text{fair value}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 1</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c4 c5\"><td>91</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-91\">Risk and return identities</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{Total risk} = \\text{systematic (market)} + \\text{unsystematic (firm-specific)}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 4, Chapter 5</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c11\"><td>92</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-92\">Expectations hypothesis (two-period illustration)</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$(1 + y_2)^2 = (1 + r_1)\\,\\bigl(1 + E[r_2]\\bigr)$$</div></td><td>Chapter 11</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c12\"><td>93</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-93\">Ownership share of a pooled fund</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{ownership share} = \\dfrac{\\text{your shares}}{\\text{shares outstanding}}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 12</td></tr><tr data-ff-ch=\"c12\"><td>94</td><td><button type=\"button\" class=\"ff-link\" data-jump=\"ff-94\">Average cost with periodic investment</button></td><td><div class=\"ff-tbase\">$$\\text{average cost} = \\dfrac{\\text{total invested}}{\\sum_t \\text{amount}_t / P_t}$$</div></td><td>Chapter 12</td></tr></tbody></table></div></details><div class=\"ff-list\"><div class=\"ff-tier\" data-ff-tier=\"1\"><h3 class=\"ff-tier-title\"><span>Tier 1</span> Must know</h3><p class=\"ff-tier-note\">The formulas that carry most of the marks in a calculation test. Learn the base formula cold, then its variants.</p><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-1\" data-ff-ch=\"c4a\" data-ff-text=\"time value of a lump sum  pv  = amount today,  r  = rate per period,  n  = number of periods. move one amount forward in time (compounding). never compare cash at different dates directly. the exponent counts compounding periods.   text rm 1 , 300  is the simple-interest answer; the extra   text rm 31  is interest on interest. present value (discounting) compounded  m  times a year effective annual rate simple interest continuous compounding doubling time (rule of 72)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">1</span><h3>Time value of a lump sum</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c4a\">Appendix 4A</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$FV = PV\\,(1+r)^n$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$PV$ = amount today, $r$ = rate per period, $n$ = number of periods.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Move one amount forward in time (compounding). Never compare cash at different dates directly.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$FV = \\text{RM}1{,}000\\,(1.10)^3 = \\text{RM}1{,}331$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>The exponent counts compounding periods. $\\text{RM}1{,}300$ is the simple-interest answer; the extra $\\text{RM}31$ is interest on interest.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Present value (discounting)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$PV = \\dfrac{FV}{(1+r)^n}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Compounded $m$ times a year</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$FV = PV\\left(1+\\dfrac{r}{m}\\right)^{mt}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Effective annual rate</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$EAR = \\left(1+\\dfrac{r}{m}\\right)^{m} - 1$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Simple interest</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$FV = PV\\,(1 + rn)$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Continuous compounding</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$EAR = e^{r} - 1$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Doubling time (rule of 72)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$n = \\dfrac{\\ln 2}{\\ln(1+r)} \\approx \\dfrac{72}{r\\%}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l4-tvm\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-2\" data-ff-ch=\"c5 c8\" data-ff-text=\"capm and the security market line  r_i  = required return,  r_f  = risk-free rate,   beta_i  = beta,  e(r_m)  = expected market return. find the return an investor should demand for a given beta; compare it with the forecast. beta multiplies the premium, not the market return.  5 % + 1.2  times 11 % = 18.2 %  is wrong; the right answer is  5 % + 1.2 ,(11 % - 5 %) = 12.2 % . market risk premium alpha (above the sml = undervalued) sml as a straight line solve for beta required return from capm (ch 8) market risk premium (ch 8)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">2</span><h3>CAPM and the Security Market Line</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c5\">Chapter 5</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c8\">Chapter 8</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$R_i = R_f + \\beta_i\\,\\bigl[E(R_m) - R_f\\bigr]$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$R_i$ = required return, $R_f$ = risk-free rate, $\\beta_i$ = beta, $E(R_m)$ = expected market return.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Find the return an investor should demand for a given beta; compare it with the forecast.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$4\\% + 1.5\\,(6\\%) = 13\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Beta multiplies the premium, not the market return. $5\\% + 1.2 \\times 11\\% = 18.2\\%$ is wrong; the right answer is $5\\% + 1.2\\,(11\\% - 5\\%) = 12.2\\%$.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Market risk premium</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$MRP = E(R_m) - R_f$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Alpha (above the SML = undervalued)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\alpha_i = E(R_i) - R_i^{\\text{CAPM}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">SML as a straight line</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_i = R_f + (\\text{slope})\\,\\beta_i,\\quad \\text{slope} = E(R_m) - R_f$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Solve for beta</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\beta_i = \\dfrac{R_i - R_f}{E(R_m) - R_f}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Required return from CAPM (Ch 8)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$k = r_f + \\beta\\,\\bigl[E(R_m) - r_f\\bigr]$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Market risk premium (Ch 8)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$MRP = E(R_m) - r_f$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l5-capm\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-3\" data-ff-ch=\"c5\" data-ff-text=\"two-asset portfolio risk  w  = weights (sum to 1),   sigma  = standard deviations,   rho_ ab   = correlation. risk of a mix of two assets. the lower the correlation, the lower   sigma_p . portfolio risk is not the weighted average of the risks (that is only true when the correlation is  +1 ). zero correlation does not mean zero risk. perfect positive correlation,   rho = +1  perfect negative correlation,   rho = -1  weights that make risk zero (  rho = -1 ) covariance correlation, always between  -1  and  +1  minimum-variance weight (extension)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">3</span><h3>Two-asset portfolio risk</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c5\">Chapter 5</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\sigma_p = \\sqrt{w_A^2\\sigma_A^2 + w_B^2\\sigma_B^2 + 2\\,w_A w_B\\,\\sigma_A\\sigma_B\\,\\rho_{AB}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$w$ = weights (sum to 1), $\\sigma$ = standard deviations, $\\rho_{AB}$ = correlation.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Risk of a mix of two assets. The lower the correlation, the lower $\\sigma_p$.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\begin{gathered} w = 0.5,\\ \\sigma_A = 20\\%,\\ \\sigma_B = 10\\%,\\ \\rho = 0 \\\\ \\sigma_p = \\sqrt{0.25(0.04) + 0.25(0.01)} = \\sqrt{0.0125} \\approx 11.18\\% \\end{gathered}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Portfolio risk is NOT the weighted average of the risks (that is only true when the correlation is $+1$). Zero correlation does not mean zero risk.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Perfect positive correlation, $\\rho = +1$</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\sigma_p = w_A\\sigma_A + w_B\\sigma_B$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Perfect negative correlation, $\\rho = -1$</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\sigma_p = \\bigl|\\,w_A\\sigma_A - w_B\\sigma_B\\,\\bigr|$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Weights that make risk zero ($\\rho = -1$)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$w_A\\sigma_A = w_B\\sigma_B \\;\\Rightarrow\\; w_A = \\dfrac{\\sigma_B}{\\sigma_A + \\sigma_B}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Covariance</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\operatorname{Cov}(R_A, R_B) = \\rho_{AB}\\,\\sigma_A\\sigma_B$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Correlation, always between $-1$ and $+1$</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\rho_{AB} = \\dfrac{\\operatorname{Cov}(R_A, R_B)}{\\sigma_A\\sigma_B}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Minimum-variance weight (extension)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$w_A^{*} = \\dfrac{\\sigma_B^2 - \\rho\\sigma_A\\sigma_B}{\\sigma_A^2 + \\sigma_B^2 - 2\\rho\\sigma_A\\sigma_B}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l5-portfolio\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-4\" data-ff-ch=\"c4 c6 c10 c11 c12\" data-ff-text=\"holding-period return (any investment)  p_0  = price paid,  p_1  = price at the end, income = dividends or interest received. return over one holding period, as a percentage of what you paid. hpr ignores time:  40 %  over  5  years is not comparable with  30 %  in  1  year. price change alone is not total return. total return in ringgit two parts of hpr capital gain (paper gain until sold) percentage return total ringgit return on  n  shares (ch 6) hpr on a position (ch 6) multi-year expected return (irr with a sale price) (ch 11) holding-period return on a bond (ch 10) income part plus price part (ch 10) return in the bond&#x27;s own currency (ch 10) fund holding period return (cash distributions) (ch 12) in ringgit for  n  shares (ch 12) table 12.2 form (ch 12) ex-distribution check (ch 12) exchange-traded investor: use market prices (ch 12)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">4</span><h3>Holding-period return (any investment)</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c4\">Chapter 4</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c6\">Chapter 6</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c10\">Chapter 10</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c11\">Chapter 11</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c12\">Chapter 12</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$HPR = \\dfrac{\\text{income} + (P_1 - P_0)}{P_0}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$P_0$ = price paid, $P_1$ = price at the end, income = dividends or interest received.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Return over one holding period, as a percentage of what you paid.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{5 + 8}{100} = 13\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>HPR ignores time: $40\\%$ over $5$ years is not comparable with $30\\%$ in $1$ year. Price change alone is not total return.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Total return in ringgit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Total return} = \\text{income} + (P_1 - P_0)$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Two parts of HPR</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$HPR = \\dfrac{\\text{income}}{P_0} + \\dfrac{P_1 - P_0}{P_0}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Capital gain (paper gain until sold)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Capital gain} = P_1 - P_0$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Percentage return</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Return} = \\dfrac{\\text{profit}}{\\text{amount invested}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Total ringgit return on $N$ shares (Ch 6)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Total return} = N\\bigl[D + (P_1 - P_0)\\bigr] = 200(0.60) + 200(22 - 20) = \\text{RM}520$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">HPR on a position (Ch 6)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$HPR = \\dfrac{\\text{dividends} + \\text{ending value} - \\text{beginning value}}{\\text{beginning value}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Multi-year expected return (IRR with a sale price) (Ch 11)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{n}\\dfrac{C}{(1+r)^t} + \\dfrac{P_n}{(1+r)^n}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Holding-period return on a bond (Ch 10)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$HPR = \\dfrac{C + (P_1 - P_0)}{P_0}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Income part plus price part (Ch 10)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$HPR = \\dfrac{C}{P_0} + \\dfrac{P_1 - P_0}{P_0} = 6.67\\% - 5.56\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Return in the bond's own currency (Ch 10)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_{\\text{USD}} = \\dfrac{980 - 1{,}000 + 50}{1{,}000} = 3\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Fund holding period return (cash distributions) (Ch 12)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$HPR = \\dfrac{(P_1 - P_0) + \\text{income dist.} + \\text{capital-gain dist.}}{P_0}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">In ringgit for $N$ shares (Ch 12)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$HPR = \\dfrac{N P_1 + N(\\text{income} + \\text{cap. gains}) - N P_0}{N P_0}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Table 12.2 form (Ch 12)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{total return} = \\dfrac{\\text{net investment income} + \\text{net gains}}{NAV_0} = \\dfrac{6.97}{24.47} = 28.48\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Ex-distribution check (Ch 12)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$NAV_{\\text{after}} = NAV_{\\text{before}} - D \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\dfrac{(NAV_{\\text{before}} - D) + D - NAV_{\\text{before}}}{NAV_{\\text{before}}} = 0\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Exchange-traded investor: use market prices (Ch 12)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$HPR = \\dfrac{(P^{\\text{mkt}}_1 - P^{\\text{mkt}}_0) + \\text{distributions}}{P^{\\text{mkt}}_0}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l4-hpr\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-5\" data-ff-ch=\"c7\" data-ff-text=\"dupont breakdown of roe  ni  = net profit after taxes. every balance on the same basis: all year-end (the slides) or all averages. explain why roe is high or changed: better margins, assets working harder, or more leverage. slide 7-43 is headed &quot;roa&quot; but breaks down roe. a higher roe that comes from a bigger equity multiplier means more debt and more risk, not better operations. mixing year-end and average balances breaks the identity. roa from margin and turnover roe from roa and leverage equity multiplier same roa, different leverage (firms a and b) averaged balances (apex) multiplier from liabilities-to-equity\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">5</span><h3>DuPont breakdown of ROE</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c7\">Chapter 7</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$ROE = \\underbrace{\\dfrac{NI}{\\text{sales}}}_{\\text{net margin}} \\times \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\text{sales}}{\\text{assets}}}_{\\text{asset turnover}} \\times \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\text{assets}}{\\text{equity}}}_{\\text{equity multiplier}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$NI$ = net profit after taxes. Every balance on the same basis: all year-end (the slides) or all averages.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Explain WHY ROE is high or changed: better margins, assets working harder, or more leverage.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$10\\% \\times 2.0 \\times \\dfrac{6}{4} = 10\\% \\times 2.0 \\times 1.5 = 30\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Slide 7-43 is headed \"ROA\" but breaks down ROE. A higher ROE that comes from a bigger equity multiplier means more debt and more risk, not better operations. Mixing year-end and average balances breaks the identity.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">ROA from margin and turnover</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$ROA = \\text{net profit margin} \\times \\text{total asset turnover} \\qquad 10\\% \\times 2 = 20\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">ROE from ROA and leverage</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$ROE = ROA \\times \\text{equity multiplier} \\qquad 20\\% \\times 1.5 = 30\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Equity multiplier</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$EM = \\dfrac{\\text{total assets}}{\\text{total stockholders' equity}} \\qquad \\dfrac{6}{4} = 1.5$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Same ROA, different leverage (Firms A and B)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$10\\% \\times \\dfrac{10}{8} = 12.5\\% \\quad\\text{vs}\\quad 10\\% \\times \\dfrac{10}{4} = 25\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Averaged balances (Apex)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{24}{300} \\times \\dfrac{300}{220} \\times \\dfrac{220}{80} = 0.08 \\times 1.3636 \\times 2.75 = 30\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Multiplier from liabilities-to-equity</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$EM = 1 + \\dfrac{\\text{total liabilities}}{\\text{equity}} \\qquad 1 + \\dfrac{160}{80} = 3.0 = \\dfrac{240}{80}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l7-dupont\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-6\" data-ff-ch=\"c7\" data-ff-text=\"return on assets and return on equity the slides use year-end balances; many analysts use the average of the beginning and ending balances. state which one you use. profit earned per ringgit of resources: all the assets (roa), or only the shareholders&#x27; money (roe). do not swap the denominators. roa and roe are accounting ratios for one period, not the stock&#x27;s return. a high roe can simply reflect a thin equity base. return on average assets (apex) return on average equity (apex) net income from roa\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">6</span><h3>Return on assets and return on equity</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c7\">Chapter 7</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$ROA = \\dfrac{\\text{net profit after taxes}}{\\text{total assets}},\\qquad ROE = \\dfrac{\\text{net profit after taxes}}{\\text{stockholders' equity}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">The slides use year-end balances; many analysts use the average of the beginning and ending balances. State which one you use.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Profit earned per ringgit of resources: all the assets (ROA), or only the shareholders' money (ROE).</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$ROA = \\dfrac{1.2}{6} = 20\\%,\\qquad ROE = \\dfrac{1.2}{4} = 30\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Do not swap the denominators. ROA and ROE are accounting ratios for one period, not the stock's return. A high ROE can simply reflect a thin equity base.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Return on average assets (Apex)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$ROA = \\dfrac{NI}{(TA_0 + TA_1)/2} = \\dfrac{24}{(200 + 240)/2} = \\dfrac{24}{220} = 10.91\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Return on average equity (Apex)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$ROE = \\dfrac{NI}{(E_0 + E_1)/2} = \\dfrac{24}{(80 + 80)/2} = 30\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Net income from ROA</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$NI = ROA \\times \\text{total assets} \\qquad 10\\% \\times 10 = 1$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l7-dupont\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-7\" data-ff-ch=\"c6 c7\" data-ff-text=\"earnings per share and the p/e ratio reported basic eps uses weighted-average common shares; the slide uses shares outstanding. trailing eps = last year; forward eps = a forecast. how many ringgit investors pay for each ringgit of earnings per share. deduct preferred dividends first. p/e divides the price per share by eps, never by total profit. negative or near-zero eps makes p/e meaningless, and a low p/e is not proof of a bargain. with preferred dividends (beacon) slide example (universal office furnishings) implied price from a multiple earnings per share (eps) (ch 6) earnings available to common (ch 6) total earnings from eps (ch 6)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">7</span><h3>Earnings per share and the P/E ratio</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c6\">Chapter 6</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c7\">Chapter 7</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$EPS = \\dfrac{NI - \\text{preferred dividends}}{\\text{common shares}},\\qquad P/E = \\dfrac{\\text{market price per share}}{EPS}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Reported basic EPS uses weighted-average common shares; the slide uses shares outstanding. Trailing EPS = last year; forward EPS = a forecast.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> How many ringgit investors pay for each ringgit of earnings per share.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$EPS = \\dfrac{\\text{RM}2\\text{m}}{1\\text{m}} = \\text{RM}2,\\qquad P/E = \\dfrac{20}{2} = 10\\times$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Deduct preferred dividends first. P/E divides the price PER SHARE by EPS, never by total profit. Negative or near-zero EPS makes P/E meaningless, and a low P/E is not proof of a bargain.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">With preferred dividends (Beacon)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$EPS = \\dfrac{25 - 1}{12} = \\text{RM}2.00,\\qquad P/E = \\dfrac{30}{2} = 15\\times$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Slide example (Universal Office Furnishings)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$EPS = \\dfrac{139.7\\text{m}}{61.815\\text{m}} = 2.26,\\qquad P/E = \\dfrac{41.50}{2.26} = 18.4$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Implied price from a multiple</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P = P/E \\times EPS \\qquad 15 \\times 2 = 30$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Earnings per share (EPS) (Ch 6)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$EPS = \\dfrac{\\text{net income available to common shareholders}}{\\text{weighted-average common shares outstanding}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Earnings available to common (Ch 6)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$NI_{\\text{common}} = \\text{net income} - \\text{preferred dividends}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Total earnings from EPS (Ch 6)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$NI_{\\text{common}} = EPS \\times \\text{shares}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l7-pershare\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-8\" data-ff-ch=\"c8\" data-ff-text=\"constant-growth dividend model (gordon)  d_0  = dividend just paid,  d_1  = next year&#x27;s expected dividend,  k  = required return,  g  = constant growth rate forever. a mature firm whose dividend is expected to grow at one steady rate forever, with the required return above that growth rate. using  d_0  instead of  d_1  ( 2/0.06 =  text rm 33.33 , or  2/0.05 =  text rm 40  instead of   text rm 42 ). if  k  le g  the formula gives no meaningful value: change the model, not the sign. next dividend from the one just paid value at a future date  n  (terminal value) dividend in any year return implied by the market price growth implied by the market price\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">8</span><h3>Constant-growth dividend model (Gordon)</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c8\">Chapter 8</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$V_0 = \\dfrac{D_1}{k - g},\\qquad D_1 = D_0\\,(1+g),\\qquad k &gt; g$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$D_0$ = dividend just paid, $D_1$ = next year's expected dividend, $k$ = required return, $g$ = constant growth rate forever.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> A mature firm whose dividend is expected to grow at one steady rate forever, with the required return above that growth rate.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$D_1 = 2(1.04) = \\text{RM}2.08,\\qquad V_0 = \\dfrac{2.08}{0.10 - 0.04} = \\text{RM}34.67$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Using $D_0$ instead of $D_1$ ($2/0.06 = \\text{RM}33.33$, or $2/0.05 = \\text{RM}40$ instead of $\\text{RM}42$). If $k \\le g$ the formula gives no meaningful value: change the model, not the sign.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Next dividend from the one just paid</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$D_1 = D_0\\,(1+g)$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Value at a future date $n$ (terminal value)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P_n = \\dfrac{D_{n+1}}{k - g}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Dividend in any year</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$D_t = D_0\\,(1+g)^t$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Return implied by the market price</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$k = \\dfrac{D_1}{P_0} + g$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Growth implied by the market price</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$g = k - \\dfrac{D_1}{P_0}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l8-ddm\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-9\" data-ff-ch=\"c10\" data-ff-text=\"conversion value of a convertible  n  = conversion ratio (shares per bond),  p_s  = current market price of one share. the value of the shares a convertible can be exchanged for right now: the starting point of every convertible calculation. conversion value is the shares&#x27; worth, not the bond&#x27;s market price, and not a guaranteed minimum: it moves with the stock. conversion ratio from the conversion price conversion price (contractual) conversion equivalent (market)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">9</span><h3>Conversion value of a convertible</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c10\">Chapter 10</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$CV = N \\times P_s$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$N$ = conversion ratio (shares per bond), $P_s$ = current market price of one share.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> The value of the shares a convertible can be exchanged for right now: the starting point of every convertible calculation.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$CV = 20 \\times \\text{RM}50 = \\text{RM}1{,}000$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Conversion value is the shares' worth, not the bond's market price, and not a guaranteed minimum: it moves with the stock.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Conversion ratio from the conversion price</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$N = \\dfrac{\\text{par}}{\\text{conversion price}} = \\dfrac{1{,}000}{40} = 25 \\text{ shares}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Conversion price (contractual)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Conversion price} = \\dfrac{\\text{par}}{N} = \\dfrac{1{,}000}{20} = \\text{RM}50$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Conversion equivalent (market)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Conversion equivalent} = \\dfrac{P_c}{N} = \\dfrac{1{,}100}{20} = \\text{RM}55$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l10-convertible\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-10\" data-ff-ch=\"c10\" data-ff-text=\"conversion premium  p_c  = market price of the convertible,  cv  = conversion value. how much more than the shares&#x27; value investors pay for the convertible&#x27;s income, option and debt features. the percentage divides by conversion value (  text rm 150 /  text rm 900 = 16.67 % ), not by the bond price ( 14.29 % ). premium as a percentage premium per share\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">10</span><h3>Conversion premium</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c10\">Chapter 10</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Premium (RM)} = P_c - CV$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$P_c$ = market price of the convertible, $CV$ = conversion value.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> How much more than the shares' value investors pay for the convertible's income, option and debt features.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$1{,}100 - 1{,}000 = \\text{RM}100,\\qquad \\dfrac{100}{1{,}000} = 10\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>The percentage divides by conversion VALUE ($\\text{RM}150 / \\text{RM}900 = 16.67\\%$), not by the bond price ($14.29\\%$).</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Premium as a percentage</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Premium (\\%)} = \\dfrac{P_c - CV}{CV}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Premium per share</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{P_c}{N} - P_s = 55 - 50 = \\text{RM}5 = \\dfrac{100}{20}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l10-convertible\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-11\" data-ff-ch=\"c1 c3 c10 c11\" data-ff-text=\"bond price (annual coupons)  c  = annual coupon (coupon rate x par),  f  = par repaid at maturity,  n  = years to maturity,  y  = required annual yield (the market yield). value a noncallable fixed-coupon bond: the present value of the coupon annuity plus the present value of par. the final cash flow is coupon plus par (  text rm 1 , 060 , not   text rm 60 ). discount each payment at its own date, never the sum of all cash flows at once. annuity form (many coupons) premium, par or discount price-yield direction zero-coupon bond annual coupon from the coupon rate longer maturity, larger price swing (ch 10) current yield (ch 1/3)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">11</span><h3>Bond price (annual coupons)</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c1\">Chapter 1</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c3\">Chapter 3</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c10\">Chapter 10</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c11\">Chapter 11</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$P_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{N}\\dfrac{C}{(1+y)^t} + \\dfrac{F}{(1+y)^N}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$C$ = annual coupon (coupon rate x par), $F$ = par repaid at maturity, $N$ = years to maturity, $y$ = required annual yield (the market yield).</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Value a noncallable fixed-coupon bond: the present value of the coupon annuity plus the present value of par.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$P_0 = \\dfrac{60}{1.08} + \\dfrac{1{,}060}{1.08^2} = 55.56 + 908.78 = \\text{RM}964.33$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>The final cash flow is coupon PLUS par ($\\text{RM}1{,}060$, not $\\text{RM}60$). Discount each payment at its own date, never the sum of all cash flows at once.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Annuity form (many coupons)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P_0 = C\\left[\\dfrac{1-(1+y)^{-N}}{y}\\right] + \\dfrac{F}{(1+y)^N}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Premium, par or discount</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{coupon rate} \\gtreqless y \\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\; P_0 \\gtreqless F$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Price-yield direction</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$y \\uparrow \\;\\Rightarrow\\; P_0 \\downarrow, \\qquad y \\downarrow \\;\\Rightarrow\\; P_0 \\uparrow$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Zero-coupon bond</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P_0 = \\dfrac{F}{(1+y)^N}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Annual coupon from the coupon rate</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$C = \\text{coupon rate} \\times F$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Longer maturity, larger price swing (Ch 10)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$n_{\\text{long}} &gt; n_{\\text{short}} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; |\\%\\Delta P_{\\text{long}}| &gt; |\\%\\Delta P_{\\text{short}}|$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Current yield (Ch 1/3)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Current yield} = \\dfrac{\\text{annual coupon}}{\\text{price}}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l11-pricing\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-12\" data-ff-ch=\"c12\" data-ff-text=\"net asset value (nav) per share assets at market value (securities, cash, other assets), liabilities = what the fund owes, all at the same valuation point. value one share of a fund; the price an open-end fund deals at; the reference point for a premium or discount. slide 12-9 divides securities by shares and leaves out liabilities:   dfrac 105  10  =  text rm 10.50  is the wrong answer. subtract liabilities first. net assets value of a holding nav roll-forward for one share (table 12.2) issuing shares at nav leaves nav unchanged the slide&#x27;s simplified form (no liabilities)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">12</span><h3>Net asset value (NAV) per share</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c12\">Chapter 12</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$NAV = \\dfrac{\\text{total assets} - \\text{total liabilities}}{\\text{shares outstanding}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Assets at market value (securities, cash, other assets), liabilities = what the fund owes, all at the same valuation point.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Value one share of a fund; the price an open-end fund deals at; the reference point for a premium or discount.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{\\text{RM}105\\text{m} - \\text{RM}5\\text{m}}{10\\text{m}} = \\text{RM}10$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Slide 12-9 divides securities by shares and leaves out liabilities: $\\dfrac{105}{10} = \\text{RM}10.50$ is the wrong answer. Subtract liabilities first.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Net assets</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{net assets} = \\text{total assets} - \\text{total liabilities}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Value of a holding</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{holding value} = \\text{shares held} \\times NAV$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">NAV roll-forward for one share (Table 12.2)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$NAV_1 = NAV_0 + \\text{net investment income} + \\text{net gains} - \\text{distributions}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Issuing shares at NAV leaves NAV unchanged</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{\\text{net assets} + n \\cdot NAV}{\\text{shares} + n} = NAV$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">The slide's simplified form (no liabilities)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$NAV \\approx \\dfrac{\\text{value of all securities}}{\\text{shares outstanding}}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l12-pool\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-13\" data-ff-ch=\"c2\" data-ff-text=\"margin  v  = current market value of the securities,  d  = loan (debit balance). check whether a leveraged position is still safe. a margin call comes when margin falls below the maintenance level. margin is not the amount borrowed. the loan does not shrink when the price falls, so margin falls faster than the price. initial equity and loan margin-call value margin-call price per share deposit that restores the margin\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">13</span><h3>Margin</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c2\">Chapter 2</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Margin} = \\dfrac{V - D}{V} = \\dfrac{\\text{investor equity}}{\\text{market value}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$V$ = current market value of the securities, $D$ = loan (debit balance).</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Check whether a leveraged position is still safe. A margin call comes when margin falls below the maintenance level.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$V = 10{,}000,\\ D = 4{,}000 \\to 60\\%;\\quad V \\to 7{,}000:\\ \\dfrac{3{,}000}{7{,}000} = 42.86\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Margin is not the amount borrowed. The loan does not shrink when the price falls, so margin falls faster than the price.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Initial equity and loan</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$E_0 = V_0\\, m_{\\text{initial}},\\qquad D = V_0\\,(1 - m_{\\text{initial}})$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Margin-call value</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$V^{*} = \\dfrac{D}{1 - m}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Margin-call price per share</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P^{*} = \\dfrac{D}{N\\,(1 - m)}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Deposit that restores the margin</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Deposit} = D - V\\,(1 - m)$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l2-margin\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-14\" data-ff-ch=\"c5\" data-ff-text=\"portfolio beta  w_i  = weight of asset  i ,   beta_i  = its beta. market sensitivity of a portfolio. unlike   sigma_p , it is a plain weighted average. do not take an unweighted average. low beta does not mean low total risk (  beta  neq  sigma ). move of the market beta from data (extension) market beta weight needed for a target beta\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">14</span><h3>Portfolio beta</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c5\">Chapter 5</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\beta_p = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i\\,\\beta_i$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$w_i$ = weight of asset $i$, $\\beta_i$ = its beta.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Market sensitivity of a portfolio. Unlike $\\sigma_p$, it is a plain weighted average.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$0.4(1.5) + 0.6(0.5) = 0.90$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Do not take an unweighted average. Low beta does not mean low total risk ($\\beta \\neq \\sigma$).</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Move of the market</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\Delta R_i \\approx \\beta_i\\,\\Delta R_m$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Beta from data (extension)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\beta_i = \\dfrac{\\operatorname{Cov}(R_i, R_m)}{\\operatorname{Var}(R_m)} = \\rho_{i,m}\\,\\dfrac{\\sigma_i}{\\sigma_m}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Market beta</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\beta_m = 1$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Weight needed for a target beta</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$w_A = \\dfrac{\\beta^{*} - \\beta_B}{\\beta_A - \\beta_B}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l5-beta\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-15\" data-ff-ch=\"c5\" data-ff-text=\"portfolio expected return  w_i  = weight,  e(r_i)  = expected return of asset  i . return of any mix of assets: always a weighted average. use the weights, not the simple mean of the returns. weight needed for a target return return in one period\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">15</span><h3>Portfolio expected return</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c5\">Chapter 5</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$E(R_p) = \\sum_{i=1}^{n} w_i\\,E(R_i),\\qquad \\sum w_i = 1$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$w_i$ = weight, $E(R_i)$ = expected return of asset $i$.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Return of any mix of assets: always a weighted average.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$0.50(12\\%) + 0.30(8\\%) + 0.20(5\\%) = 9.4\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Use the weights, not the simple mean of the returns.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Weight needed for a target return</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$w_A = \\dfrac{R^{*} - R_B}{R_A - R_B}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Return in one period</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_{p,t} = \\sum_i w_i R_{i,t}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l5-portfolio\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-16\" data-ff-ch=\"c4 c11\" data-ff-text=\"required return  ip  = inflation premium,  rp  = risk premium,  r_f  = risk-free rate. the hurdle an investment must clear to be worth its risk. do not leave out the inflation premium, and do not confuse the risk premium with the total return. risk-free rate hurdle test required return on a bond (ch 11) exact fisher relationship (ch 11) exact vs additive (ch 11)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">16</span><h3>Required return</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c4\">Chapter 4</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c11\">Chapter 11</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$R_{\\text{required}} = R_{\\text{real}} + IP + RP = R_f + RP$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$IP$ = inflation premium, $RP$ = risk premium, $R_f$ = risk-free rate.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> The hurdle an investment must clear to be worth its risk.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$2\\% + 3\\% + 4\\% = 9\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Do not leave out the inflation premium, and do not confuse the risk premium with the total return.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Risk-free rate</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_f = R_{\\text{real}} + IP$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Hurdle test</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$E(R) - R_{\\text{required}} \\ge 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{acceptable}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Required return on a bond (Ch 11)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$r \\approx r_{\\text{real}} + IP + RP$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Exact Fisher relationship (Ch 11)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$(1 + r_n) = (1 + r_{\\text{real}})(1 + \\pi)$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Exact vs additive (Ch 11)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$1.02 \\times 1.03 - 1 = 5.06\\%\\ \\text{vs}\\ 2\\% + 3\\% = 5\\%$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l4-return\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-17\" data-ff-ch=\"c2 c6 c10 c12\" data-ff-text=\"home-currency return (foreign shares, bonds, funds)  r_ fx   = change in the exchange rate, from the home investor&#x27;s point of view. return on a foreign investment as the home investor sees it. do not simply add the asset return and the currency move. convert every cash flow, then compare (safest) (ch 6) currency return ( s  = rm per foreign unit) (ch 6) additive approximation (slide 44) (ch 6) interaction term (ch 6) directly in ringgit (ch 10) currency change (ch 10)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">17</span><h3>Home-currency return (foreign shares, bonds, funds)</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c2\">Chapter 2</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c6\">Chapter 6</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c10\">Chapter 10</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c12\">Chapter 12</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$(1 + R_{\\text{home}}) = (1 + R_{\\text{asset}})\\,(1 + R_{FX})$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$R_{FX}$ = change in the exchange rate, from the home investor's point of view.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Return on a foreign investment as the home investor sees it.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$(1.10)(0.88) - 1 = -3.2\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Do not simply add the asset return and the currency move.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Convert every cash flow, then compare (safest) (Ch 6)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_{\\text{RM}} = \\dfrac{(P_1 + D)\\,S_1 - P_0 S_0}{P_0 S_0} = \\dfrac{(110 + 2)(3.80) - 400}{400} = 6.4\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Currency return ($S$ = RM per foreign unit) (Ch 6)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_{\\text{currency}} = \\dfrac{S_1}{S_0} - 1 = \\dfrac{3.80}{4.00} - 1 = -5\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Additive approximation (slide 44) (Ch 6)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_{\\text{home}} \\approx R_{\\text{local}} + R_{\\text{currency}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Interaction term (Ch 6)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_{\\text{home}} - (R_{\\text{local}} + R_{\\text{currency}}) = R_{\\text{local}} \\times R_{\\text{currency}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Directly in ringgit (Ch 10)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_{\\text{RM}} = \\dfrac{(P_1 + C)\\,S_1}{P_0\\,S_0} - 1 = \\dfrac{1{,}030 \\times 3.80}{1{,}000 \\times 4.00} - 1 = -2.15\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Currency change (Ch 10)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\Delta FX = \\dfrac{S_1}{S_0} - 1 = \\dfrac{3.80}{4.00} - 1 = -5\\%$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l2-global\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-18\" data-ff-ch=\"c6 c7\" data-ff-text=\"dividend yield  d  = annual cash dividend per share,  p  = current market price per share. current cash income per ringgit invested at today&#x27;s price; compare income with other investments. dividing by eps (that is the payout ratio). a yield that rises because the price fell (  dfrac 0.80  10  = 8 % ) is not a better or safer investment, and a yield is not the total return. yield after a price change, same dividend price falls by a fraction  x  annual dividend from quarterly payments\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">18</span><h3>Dividend yield</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c6\">Chapter 6</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c7\">Chapter 7</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Dividend yield} = \\dfrac{\\text{annual dividend per share}}{\\text{current share price}} = \\dfrac{D}{P}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$D$ = annual cash dividend per share, $P$ = current market price per share.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Current cash income per ringgit invested at today's price; compare income with other investments.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{0.80}{20} = 4\\%,\\qquad \\dfrac{1.20}{24} = 5\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Dividing by EPS (that is the payout ratio). A yield that rises because the price fell ($\\dfrac{0.80}{10} = 8\\%$) is not a better or safer investment, and a yield is not the total return.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Yield after a price change, same dividend</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{new yield} = \\dfrac{D}{P_{\\text{new}}} = \\dfrac{0.80}{16} = 5\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Price falls by a fraction $x$</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{new yield} = \\dfrac{\\text{old yield}}{1 - x}:\\quad x = 20\\% \\Rightarrow \\text{yield} \\times 1.25$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Annual dividend from quarterly payments</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$D = 4 \\times d_{\\text{quarterly}}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l6-dividends\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-19\" data-ff-ch=\"c6 c7\" data-ff-text=\"dividend payout ratio and dividends per share dividend per share and  eps  for the same (annual) period. what share of the year&#x27;s earnings is distributed; the rest is retained. dividing by the share price (that is the yield). with  eps  le 0  the ratio is undefined or misleading; above  100 %  the company pays more than it earned, which needs investigation, not an automatic verdict. retention ratio dividend from payout and eps dividends per share, payout and dividend yield (ch 7) dividends per share (beacon) (ch 7) retention ratio (ch 7) dividend yield (tables 7.6 and 7.7) (ch 7)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">19</span><h3>Dividend payout ratio and dividends per share</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c6\">Chapter 6</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c7\">Chapter 7</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Payout ratio} = \\dfrac{\\text{dividend per share}}{EPS}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Dividend per share and $EPS$ for the same (annual) period.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> What share of the year's earnings is distributed; the rest is retained.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{0.80}{2.00} = 40\\%\\ \\text{paid},\\ 60\\%\\ \\text{retained}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Dividing by the share price (that is the yield). With $EPS \\le 0$ the ratio is undefined or misleading; above $100\\%$ the company pays more than it earned, which needs investigation, not an automatic verdict.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Retention ratio</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Retention} = 1 - \\text{payout} = \\dfrac{EPS - D}{EPS}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Dividend from payout and EPS</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$D = \\text{payout} \\times EPS = 30\\% \\times 4 = \\text{RM}1.20$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Dividends per share, payout and dividend yield (Ch 7)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$DPS = \\dfrac{\\text{annual dividends to common}}{\\text{common shares}},\\qquad \\text{Payout} = \\dfrac{DPS}{EPS}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Dividends per share (Beacon) (Ch 7)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$DPS = \\dfrac{9.6}{12} = \\text{RM}0.80$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Retention ratio (Ch 7)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$b = 1 - \\text{payout} = 1 - 40\\% = 60\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Dividend yield (Tables 7.6 and 7.7) (Ch 7)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Dividend yield} = \\dfrac{DPS}{\\text{price}} \\qquad \\dfrac{0.80}{30} = 2.67\\%$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l6-dividends\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-20\" data-ff-ch=\"c7\" data-ff-text=\"current ratio and net working capital current = due or expected to turn into cash within about a year. the ratio is in &quot;times&quot;; working capital is in ringgit. short-term liquidity: how many ringgit of current assets back each ringgit owed soon. a rise built on slow-moving inventory or doubtful receivables is not better liquidity. the ratio can fall while working capital rises (apex:  2.00  to 1.56  but  40  to 45 ). net working capital current assets from their parts (apex, year-end) quick (acid-test) ratio: extension, not on the slides\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">20</span><h3>Current ratio and net working capital</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c7\">Chapter 7</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Current ratio} = \\dfrac{\\text{current assets}}{\\text{current liabilities}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Current = due or expected to turn into cash within about a year. The ratio is in \"times\"; working capital is in ringgit.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Short-term liquidity: how many ringgit of current assets back each ringgit owed soon.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{3}{2} = 1.5\\times,\\qquad NWC = 3 - 2 = \\text{RM}1\\text{ million}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>A rise built on slow-moving inventory or doubtful receivables is not better liquidity. The ratio can fall while working capital rises (Apex: $2.00 \\to 1.56$ but $40 \\to 45$).</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Net working capital</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$NWC = \\text{current assets} - \\text{current liabilities} \\qquad 600{,}000 - 300{,}000 = \\text{RM}300{,}000$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Current assets from their parts (Apex, year-end)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$CA = 15 + 50 + 60 = 125,\\qquad \\dfrac{125}{80} = 1.56$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Quick (acid-test) ratio: extension, not on the slides</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Quick ratio} = \\dfrac{CA - \\text{inventory}}{CL} \\qquad \\dfrac{4.8 - 2.5}{3.4} = 0.68$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l7-ratios\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-21\" data-ff-ch=\"c7\" data-ff-text=\"receivables turnover and collection period slide definition: sales over year-end receivables. a common alternative: credit sales over average receivables. how quickly customers pay. falling turnover means cash is tied up in unpaid invoices. use the stated numerator and denominator. receivables growing faster than sales is a warning sign to investigate, not proof of bad debts or fraud. credit sales over average receivables average collection period average receivables (apex)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">21</span><h3>Receivables turnover and collection period</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c7\">Chapter 7</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Receivables turnover} = \\dfrac{\\text{annual sales}}{\\text{accounts receivable}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Slide definition: sales over year-end receivables. A common alternative: credit sales over average receivables.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> How quickly customers pay. Falling turnover means cash is tied up in unpaid invoices.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{12}{2} = 6\\times,\\qquad \\dfrac{365}{6} = 60.8\\ \\text{days}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Use the stated numerator and denominator. Receivables growing faster than sales is a warning sign to investigate, not proof of bad debts or fraud.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Credit sales over average receivables</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{\\text{credit sales}}{(AR_0 + AR_1)/2} \\qquad \\dfrac{9}{1.5} = 6\\times$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Average collection period</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Days} \\approx \\dfrac{365}{\\text{receivables turnover}} \\qquad \\dfrac{365}{7.5} = 48.67\\ \\text{days}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Average receivables (Apex)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\overline{AR} = \\dfrac{30 + 50}{2} = 40,\\qquad \\dfrac{300}{40} = 7.5\\times$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l7-ratios\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-22\" data-ff-ch=\"c7\" data-ff-text=\"net profit margin ringgit of net profit kept from each ringgit of sales. profitability of sales; the first dupont driver. divide profit by sales, not by assets or equity. an unchanged margin can hide weakening cash flow (integrated case: sales and profit both up  20 % , margin still  10 % ). net income from margin and sales net profit after taxes (apex)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">22</span><h3>Net profit margin</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c7\">Chapter 7</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Net profit margin} = \\dfrac{\\text{net profit after taxes}}{\\text{total revenues}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Ringgit of net profit kept from each ringgit of sales.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Profitability of sales; the first DuPont driver.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{1.2}{12} = 10\\%\\quad(\\text{RM}0.10 \\text{ per } \\text{RM}1 \\text{ of sales})$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Divide profit by SALES, not by assets or equity. An unchanged margin can hide weakening cash flow (integrated case: sales and profit both up $20\\%$, margin still $10\\%$).</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Net income from margin and sales</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$NI = \\text{margin} \\times \\text{sales} \\qquad 5\\% \\times 2{,}000{,}000 = \\text{RM}100{,}000$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Net profit after taxes (Apex)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$NI = EBIT - \\text{interest} - \\text{tax} \\qquad 40 - 10 - 6 = 24$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l7-dupont\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-23\" data-ff-ch=\"c8\" data-ff-text=\"two-stage (variable-growth) dividend model  d_t = d_0(1+g_1)^t  during the  n  high-growth years,  g_2  = long-run growth from year  n+1 ,  p_n  = terminal value dated at year  n . unusual growth for a few years, then stable growth forever.  p_n  is a value at year  n , not today: discount it  n  years. build it from  d_ n+1   (not  d_n ), add  d_n  separately, and do not add  d_ n+1 , d_ n+2 ,  dots  again. high-growth dividends first stable dividend terminal value at the transition date present value of the terminal value\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">23</span><h3>Two-stage (variable-growth) dividend model</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c8\">Chapter 8</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$V_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{n}\\dfrac{D_t}{(1+k)^t} + \\dfrac{P_n}{(1+k)^n},\\qquad P_n = \\dfrac{D_{n+1}}{k - g_2}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$D_t = D_0(1+g_1)^t$ during the $n$ high-growth years, $g_2$ = long-run growth from year $n+1$, $P_n$ = terminal value dated at year $n$.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Unusual growth for a few years, then stable growth forever.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\begin{gathered} D_1 = 1.20,\\ D_2 = 1.44,\\ D_3 = 1.4976,\\ P_2 = \\dfrac{1.4976}{0.12 - 0.04} = 18.72 \\\\ V_0 = \\dfrac{1.20}{1.12} + \\dfrac{1.44 + 18.72}{1.12^2} \\approx \\text{RM}17.14 \\end{gathered}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>$P_n$ is a value at year $n$, not today: discount it $n$ years. Build it from $D_{n+1}$ (not $D_n$), add $D_n$ separately, and do not add $D_{n+1}, D_{n+2}, \\dots$ again.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">High-growth dividends</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$D_t = D_0\\,(1+g_1)^t,\\quad t = 1, \\dots, n$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">First stable dividend</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$D_{n+1} = D_n\\,(1+g_2)$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Terminal value at the transition date</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P_n = \\dfrac{D_{n+1}}{k - g_2}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Present value of the terminal value</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$PV(P_n) = \\dfrac{P_n}{(1+k)^n}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l8-variable\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-24\" data-ff-ch=\"c8\" data-ff-text=\"forecast chain: sales to eps, dividend and future price  g_s  = forecast sales growth, net margin = net profit ÷ sales, shares = expected common shares outstanding (no preferred dividends). turning a business forecast into per-share benefits: the dividend and the year-end price. payout is the share paid out, not retained. more shares (dilution) cut eps even when profit is unchanged.  p_1  is a year-end price, not today&#x27;s value. forecast sales forecast net profit forecast dividend per share forecast year-end price retention ratio\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">24</span><h3>Forecast chain: sales to EPS, dividend and future price</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c8\">Chapter 8</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$EPS_1 = \\dfrac{\\text{Sales}_0\\,(1+g_s) \\times \\text{net margin}}{\\text{common shares}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$g_s$ = forecast sales growth, net margin = net profit ÷ sales, shares = expected common shares outstanding (no preferred dividends).</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Turning a business forecast into per-share benefits: the dividend and the year-end price.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{100(1.08)(0.06)}{2} = \\dfrac{6.48}{2} = \\text{RM}3.24,\\quad D_1 = 3.24(0.40) = 1.296,\\quad P_1 = 3.24(17.5) = 56.70$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Payout is the share paid OUT, not retained. More shares (dilution) cut EPS even when profit is unchanged. $P_1$ is a year-end price, not today's value.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Forecast sales</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Sales}_1 = \\text{Sales}_0\\,(1+g_s)$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Forecast net profit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Net profit}_1 = \\text{Sales}_1 \\times \\text{net margin}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Forecast dividend per share</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$D_1 = EPS_1 \\times \\text{payout ratio}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Forecast year-end price</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P_1 = EPS_1 \\times (P/E)_1$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Retention ratio</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$b = 1 - \\text{payout ratio}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l8-forecast\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-25\" data-ff-ch=\"c8\" data-ff-text=\"value of dividends plus a future sale price  d_t  = dividend at the end of year  t ,  p_n  = expected sale price at year  n  (after the year- n  dividend),  k  = required return. a planned holding period: discount every dividend and the sale price to today, then compare with today&#x27;s price. comparing a future price with today&#x27;s price directly (  text rm 23 &lt;  text rm 24 ) ignores the dividend, the wait and  k . the year- n  dividend and  p_n  are both discounted  n  years. one-year holding period present value of one future amount decision rule\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">25</span><h3>Value of dividends plus a future sale price</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c8\">Chapter 8</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$V_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{N}\\dfrac{D_t}{(1+k)^t} + \\dfrac{P_N}{(1+k)^N}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$D_t$ = dividend at the end of year $t$, $P_N$ = expected sale price at year $N$ (after the year-$N$ dividend), $k$ = required return.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> A planned holding period: discount every dividend and the sale price to today, then compare with today's price.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$V_0 = \\dfrac{1.20 + 24}{1.12} = \\text{RM}22.50;\\qquad \\dfrac{1.20}{1.12} + \\dfrac{1.50 + 18.75}{1.12^2} = \\text{RM}17.21$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Comparing a future price with today's price directly ($\\text{RM}23 &lt; \\text{RM}24$) ignores the dividend, the wait and $k$. The year-$N$ dividend and $P_N$ are both discounted $N$ years.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">One-year holding period</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$V_0 = \\dfrac{D_1 + P_1}{1+k}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Present value of one future amount</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$PV = \\dfrac{CF_n}{(1+k)^n}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Decision rule</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$V_0 &gt; P_0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{potentially undervalued (under the assumptions)}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l8-required\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-26\" data-ff-ch=\"c9\" data-ff-text=\"advance-decline: net breadth and the a-d line  a_t  = number of advancing issues,  d_t  = number of declining issues on day  t ;  l_t  = level of the advance-decline line. count how many stocks took part in a move: an index can rise on a few large stocks while most stocks fall. net breadth is the daily difference; the a-d line is its running total. in mcq q23 add today&#x27;s  +400  to yesterday&#x27;s  5 , 000 :  5 , 400 , not  400 . a-d line: add today&#x27;s net breadth to yesterday&#x27;s level several days at once\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">26</span><h3>Advance-decline: net breadth and the A-D line</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c9\">Chapter 9</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Net breadth}_t = A_t - D_t$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$A_t$ = number of advancing issues, $D_t$ = number of declining issues on day $t$; $L_t$ = level of the advance-decline line.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Count how many stocks took part in a move: an index can rise on a few large stocks while most stocks fall.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$1{,}000 + (800 - 600) = 1{,}200;\\quad 1{,}200 + (500 - 750) = 950$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Net breadth is the daily difference; the A-D line is its running total. In MCQ Q23 add today's $+400$ to yesterday's $5{,}000$: $5{,}400$, not $400$.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">A-D line: add today's net breadth to yesterday's level</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$L_t = L_{t-1} + (A_t - D_t)$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Several days at once</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$L_T = L_0 + \\sum_{t=1}^{T} (A_t - D_t)$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l9-indicators\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-27\" data-ff-ch=\"c9\" data-ff-text=\"trin (arms index)  a ,  d  = advancing and declining issues;  av ,  dv  = volume traded in advancing and declining issues. compare breadth (issues) with the volume behind it. the slides read lower values as bullish and higher values as bearish. a ratio of two ratios: issues on top, volume underneath. flipping the volume ratio turns mcq q26&#x27;s  0.50  into  4.50 . check that  d ,  av  and  dv  are not zero. cross-multiplied form volume per issue: why low trin reads as bullish neutral reading\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">27</span><h3>TRIN (Arms Index)</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c9\">Chapter 9</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{TRIN} = \\dfrac{A / D}{AV / DV}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$A$, $D$ = advancing and declining issues; $AV$, $DV$ = volume traded in advancing and declining issues.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Compare breadth (issues) with the volume behind it. The slides read lower values as bullish and higher values as bearish.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{800/400}{600/200} = \\dfrac{2}{3} \\approx 0.67$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>A ratio of two ratios: issues on top, volume underneath. Flipping the volume ratio turns MCQ Q26's $0.50$ into $4.50$. Check that $D$, $AV$ and $DV$ are not zero.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Cross-multiplied form</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{TRIN} = \\dfrac{A \\times DV}{D \\times AV}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Volume per issue: why low TRIN reads as bullish</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{TRIN} = \\dfrac{DV/D}{AV/A} &lt; 1 \\iff \\dfrac{AV}{A} &gt; \\dfrac{DV}{D}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Neutral reading</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{TRIN} = 1 \\iff \\dfrac{A}{D} = \\dfrac{AV}{DV}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l9-indicators\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-28\" data-ff-ch=\"c9\" data-ff-text=\"simple moving average  p_t  = closing price on day  t ,  n  = number of closes in the window (the slides mention  10  to  200  days). smooth daily noise to see a trend; a specified rule may treat the price crossing the average as a signal. use the last  n  closes ending today: the day 5 three-day average in subjective q5 uses days 3-5,  (10 + 12 + 13)/3 =  text rm 11.67 . the average is not the latest price, and it cannot forecast. roll forward one day: drop the oldest close, add the newest crossover rule (a specified signal, not proof of a trend)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">28</span><h3>Simple moving average</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c9\">Chapter 9</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$SMA_n = \\dfrac{P_t + P_{t-1} + \\cdots + P_{t-n+1}}{n}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$P_t$ = closing price on day $t$, $n$ = number of closes in the window (the slides mention $10$ to $200$ days).</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Smooth daily noise to see a trend; a specified rule may treat the price crossing the average as a signal.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{10 + 11 + 12 + 11 + 16}{5} = \\text{RM}12$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Use the last $n$ closes ending today: the Day 5 three-day average in subjective Q5 uses Days 3-5, $(10 + 12 + 13)/3 = \\text{RM}11.67$. The average is not the latest price, and it cannot forecast.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Roll forward one day: drop the oldest close, add the newest</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$SMA_t = SMA_{t-1} + \\dfrac{P_t - P_{t-n}}{n}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Crossover rule (a specified signal, not proof of a trend)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P_{t-1} \\le SMA_{t-1} \\text{ and } P_t &gt; SMA_t \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{possible buy signal}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l9-charts\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-29\" data-ff-ch=\"c10\" data-ff-text=\"taxable-equivalent yield  y_  text exempt    = tax-exempt (municipal) yield,  t  = the investor&#x27;s marginal tax rate on taxable interest. compare a tax-exempt bond with a taxable one on the same footing. buy the exempt bond if its tey exceeds the taxable yield. divide by  (1 - t) ; do not multiply.  4 %  times 0.8 = 3.2 %  answers a different question. the comparison assumes equal credit, maturity and liquidity. after-tax yield of a taxable bond break-even tax rate federal and state taxes (slide 10-24) tax-exempt yield that matches a taxable one\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">29</span><h3>Taxable-equivalent yield</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c10\">Chapter 10</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$TEY = \\dfrac{y_{\\text{exempt}}}{1 - t}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$y_{\\text{exempt}}$ = tax-exempt (municipal) yield, $t$ = the investor's marginal tax rate on taxable interest.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Compare a tax-exempt bond with a taxable one on the same footing. Buy the exempt bond if its TEY exceeds the taxable yield.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{4\\%}{1 - 0.20} = 5\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Divide by $(1 - t)$; do not multiply. $4\\% \\times 0.8 = 3.2\\%$ answers a different question. The comparison assumes equal credit, maturity and liquidity.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">After-tax yield of a taxable bond</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$y_{\\text{after-tax}} = y_{\\text{taxable}}\\,(1 - t) = 6\\% \\times 0.72 = 4.32\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Break-even tax rate</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$t^{*} = 1 - \\dfrac{y_{\\text{exempt}}}{y_{\\text{taxable}}} = 1 - \\dfrac{4.2\\%}{6\\%} = 30\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Federal and state taxes (slide 10-24)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$TEY = \\dfrac{y_{\\text{muni}}}{1 - [\\,t_f + t_s(1 - t_f)\\,]}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Tax-exempt yield that matches a taxable one</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$y_{\\text{exempt}} = TEY \\times (1 - t)$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l10-issuers\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-30\" data-ff-ch=\"c10 c11\" data-ff-text=\"current yield annual coupon in ringgit divided by the price paid today. annual coupon income per ringgit invested at today&#x27;s price. not the total return and not ytm: it ignores the gain or loss to maturity and reinvestment. price below par means current yield above the coupon rate. price implied by a current yield premium or discount test coupon rate (ch 11) current yield of a bond at par (ch 11)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">30</span><h3>Current yield</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c10\">Chapter 10</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c11\">Chapter 11</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Current yield} = \\dfrac{\\text{annual coupon}}{\\text{current market price}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Annual coupon in ringgit divided by the price paid today.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Annual coupon income per ringgit invested at today's price.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{60}{900} = 6.67\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Not the total return and not YTM: it ignores the gain or loss to maturity and reinvestment. Price below par means current yield above the coupon rate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Price implied by a current yield</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P = \\dfrac{\\text{annual coupon}}{\\text{current yield}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Premium or discount test</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P &lt; \\text{par} \\iff \\text{current yield} &gt; \\text{coupon rate}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Coupon rate (Ch 11)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{coupon rate} = \\dfrac{C}{F}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Current yield of a bond at par (Ch 11)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P_0 = F \\;\\Rightarrow\\; CY = \\text{coupon rate}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l10-basics\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-31\" data-ff-ch=\"c10\" data-ff-text=\"annual coupon coupon rate is a percentage of par value (usually   text rm 1 , 000 ). turn a quoted coupon rate into the ringgit interest the bond pays: the first step of almost every bond calculation. apply the coupon rate to par, never to the market price. the coupon does not change when the price changes. semiannual payment coupon rate from the coupon\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">31</span><h3>Annual coupon</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c10\">Chapter 10</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$C = \\text{coupon rate} \\times \\text{par}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Coupon rate is a percentage of par value (usually $\\text{RM}1{,}000$).</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Turn a quoted coupon rate into the ringgit interest the bond pays: the first step of almost every bond calculation.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$6\\% \\times 1{,}000 = \\text{RM}60$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Apply the coupon rate to PAR, never to the market price. The coupon does not change when the price changes.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Semiannual payment</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{C}{2} = \\dfrac{60}{2} = \\text{RM}30 \\text{ every six months}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Coupon rate from the coupon</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{coupon rate} = \\dfrac{C}{\\text{par}}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l10-basics\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-32\" data-ff-ch=\"c11\" data-ff-text=\"bond price (semiannual coupons)  c/2  = coupon each half-year,  y/2  = half the nominal annual yield (compounded semiannually),  2n  = number of half-years. coupons paid twice a year and the yield quoted as a nominal annual rate compounded semiannually. halve, halve, double. convert all three inputs together. discounting at  10 %  per half-year, or using only two periods, gives a wrong price (  text rm 809.81  instead of   text rm 964.54  in the mcq example). periodic inputs effective annual yield from a nominal semiannual yield six-month rate from an effective annual yield general:  m  payments a year\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">32</span><h3>Bond price (semiannual coupons)</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c11\">Chapter 11</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$P_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{2N}\\dfrac{C/2}{(1+y/2)^t} + \\dfrac{F}{(1+y/2)^{2N}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$C/2$ = coupon each half-year, $y/2$ = half the nominal annual yield (compounded semiannually), $2N$ = number of half-years.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Coupons paid twice a year and the yield quoted as a nominal annual rate compounded semiannually. Halve, halve, double.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\sum_{t=1}^{4}\\dfrac{30}{1.04^t} + \\dfrac{1{,}000}{1.04^4} \\approx \\text{RM}963.70$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Convert all three inputs together. Discounting at $10\\%$ per half-year, or using only two periods, gives a wrong price ($\\text{RM}809.81$ instead of $\\text{RM}964.54$ in the MCQ example).</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Periodic inputs</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$C_p = \\dfrac{C}{2},\\qquad i = \\dfrac{y}{2},\\qquad n = 2N$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Effective annual yield from a nominal semiannual yield</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$EAY = \\left(1+\\dfrac{y}{2}\\right)^2 - 1 = 1.05^2 - 1 = 10.25\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Six-month rate from an effective annual yield</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$i = (1+y_{\\text{eff}})^{1/2} - 1$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">General: $m$ payments a year</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{mN}\\dfrac{C/m}{(1+y/m)^t} + \\dfrac{F}{(1+y/m)^{mN}}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l11-pricing\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-33\" data-ff-ch=\"c11\" data-ff-text=\"yield to maturity (ytm)  p_0  = current market price; solve for  ytm , the internal rate of return of the promised coupons and par. the yield implied by today&#x27;s price if the bond is held to maturity and every payment is made. solve by trial and error, a calculator or a spreadsheet. ytm is a promised yield, not a guaranteed return: it assumes payment in full, holding to maturity and reinvesting coupons at the ytm. price below par means ytm above the coupon rate. ordering by price bond priced at par semiannual quote quick approximation (a shortcut, not in the notes)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">33</span><h3>Yield to maturity (YTM)</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c11\">Chapter 11</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$P_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{N}\\dfrac{C}{(1+YTM)^t} + \\dfrac{F}{(1+YTM)^N}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$P_0$ = current market price; solve for $YTM$, the internal rate of return of the promised coupons and par.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> The yield implied by today's price if the bond is held to maturity and every payment is made. Solve by trial and error, a calculator or a spreadsheet.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\begin{gathered} 809.50 = \\sum_{t=1}^{15}\\dfrac{75}{(1+YTM)^t} + \\dfrac{1{,}000}{(1+YTM)^{15}} \\ \\Rightarrow\\; YTM \\approx 10\\% \\end{gathered}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>YTM is a promised yield, not a guaranteed return: it assumes payment in full, holding to maturity and reinvesting coupons at the YTM. Price below par means YTM above the coupon rate.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Ordering by price</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\begin{gathered} P_0 &lt; F:\\ \\text{coupon rate} &lt; CY &lt; YTM \\ P_0 &gt; F:\\ \\text{coupon rate} &gt; CY &gt; YTM \\end{gathered}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Bond priced at par</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P_0 = F \\;\\Rightarrow\\; YTM = \\text{coupon rate} = CY$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Semiannual quote</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$YTM_{\\text{annual, nominal}} = 2 \\times i_{\\text{half-year}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Quick approximation (a shortcut, not in the notes)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$YTM \\approx \\dfrac{C + (F - P_0)/N}{(F + P_0)/2}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l11-yields\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-34\" data-ff-ch=\"c11\" data-ff-text=\"macaulay duration  pv(cf_t)  = present value of the cash flow at time  t ,  p_0  = bond price (the sum of the pvs). the weights  pv(cf_t)/p_0  add to  1 . the present-value-weighted average time to receive a bond&#x27;s cash flows, in years. needed for modified duration and for immunization. weight each time by present value, not by the raw cash flow. duration is usually shorter than maturity; it equals maturity only for a zero-coupon bond. pv weight of each cash flow zero-coupon bond half-year periods portfolio duration (value-weighted)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">34</span><h3>Macaulay duration</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c11\">Chapter 11</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$D_{Mac} = \\sum_{t=1}^{N} t \\times \\dfrac{PV(CF_t)}{P_0}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$PV(CF_t)$ = present value of the cash flow at time $t$, $P_0$ = bond price (the sum of the PVs). The weights $PV(CF_t)/P_0$ add to $1$.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> The present-value-weighted average time to receive a bond's cash flows, in years. Needed for modified duration and for immunization.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$D_{Mac} = \\dfrac{1(55.56) + 2(908.78)}{964.33} = 1.94239\\ \\text{years}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Weight each time by PRESENT value, not by the raw cash flow. Duration is usually shorter than maturity; it equals maturity only for a zero-coupon bond.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">PV weight of each cash flow</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$w_t = \\dfrac{CF_t/(1+y)^t}{P_0},\\qquad \\sum_t w_t = 1$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Zero-coupon bond</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$D_{Mac} = N$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Half-year periods</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$D_{Mac}\\ (\\text{years}) = \\dfrac{D_{Mac}\\ (\\text{half-years})}{2}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Portfolio duration (value-weighted)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$D_p = \\sum_j \\dfrac{V_j}{V_p}\\,D_j$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l11-duration\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-35\" data-ff-ch=\"c11\" data-ff-text=\"modified duration and price sensitivity  y  = annual yield (annual-pay bond),   delta y  = change in yield as a decimal ( 1  percentage point  = 0.01 ). estimate the percentage price change for a small yield change; compare the rate sensitivity of bonds. write   delta y  in decimals:  0.20  percentage point is  0.002 , not  0.20 . the estimate is linear; large moves and convexity make the actual change differ. change in ringgit  m  payments a year new price estimate\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">35</span><h3>Modified duration and price sensitivity</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c11\">Chapter 11</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$D_{mod} = \\dfrac{D_{Mac}}{1+y},\\quad \\dfrac{\\Delta P}{P} \\approx -D_{mod}\\,\\Delta y$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$y$ = annual yield (annual-pay bond), $\\Delta y$ = change in yield as a decimal ($1$ percentage point $= 0.01$).</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Estimate the percentage price change for a SMALL yield change; compare the rate sensitivity of bonds.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$D_{mod} = \\dfrac{1.94239}{1.08} = 1.7985;\\quad -1.7985 \\times 0.01 = -1.80\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Write $\\Delta y$ in decimals: $0.20$ percentage point is $0.002$, not $0.20$. The estimate is linear; large moves and convexity make the actual change differ.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Change in ringgit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\Delta P \\approx -D_{mod} \\times \\Delta y \\times P_0$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">$m$ payments a year</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$D_{mod} = \\dfrac{D_{Mac}}{1 + y/m}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">New price estimate</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P_1 \\approx P_0\\,(1 - D_{mod}\\,\\Delta y)$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l11-duration\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-36\" data-ff-ch=\"c12\" data-ff-text=\"premium or discount to nav positive = premium (price above nav), negative = discount. applies to closed-end funds and etfs. a closed-end fund or etf trades on an exchange and you compare its price with the value of its net assets per share. divide by nav, not by the price: nav   text rm 20 , price   text rm 21  is a  5 %  premium, not  4.76 % . a discount is an observation, not proof of a bargain. premium or discount in ringgit price from nav and premium  d  etf example from the integrated case\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">36</span><h3>Premium or discount to NAV</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c12\">Chapter 12</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{premium or discount} = \\dfrac{\\text{market price} - NAV}{NAV}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Positive = premium (price above NAV), negative = discount. Applies to closed-end funds and ETFs.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> A closed-end fund or ETF trades on an exchange and you compare its price with the value of its net assets per share.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{9 - 10}{10} = -10\\%\\ \\text{(a } 10\\%\\ \\text{discount)};\\qquad \\dfrac{10.50 - 10}{10} = +5\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Divide by NAV, not by the price: NAV $\\text{RM}20$, price $\\text{RM}21$ is a $5\\%$ premium, not $4.76\\%$. A discount is an observation, not proof of a bargain.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Premium or discount in ringgit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P - NAV$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Price from NAV and premium $d$</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P = NAV\\,(1 + d)$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">ETF example from the integrated case</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{10.02 - 10}{10} = 0.2\\%\\ \\text{premium}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l12-structures\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-37\" data-ff-ch=\"c12\" data-ff-text=\"front-end load  l  = load as a fraction of the amount paid (the offering price). the rest buys shares at nav. a load fund charges a commission when shares are bought. measure the return on the full amount paid, not on the amount invested. check the stated base: &quot;% of offering price&quot; and &quot;% of amount invested&quot; give different answers. load in ringgit shares bought offer price (load as % of offer price) same load as a % of nav return on the outlay after the load load quoted as % of amount invested\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">37</span><h3>Front-end load</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c12\">Chapter 12</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{amount invested} = \\text{amount paid} \\times (1 - L)$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$L$ = load as a fraction of the amount paid (the offering price). The rest buys shares at NAV.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> A load fund charges a commission when shares are bought.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$10{,}000 \\times (1 - 0.03) = \\text{RM}9{,}700;\\quad 9{,}700 \\times 1.05 = \\text{RM}10{,}185 \\to 1.85\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Measure the return on the full amount paid, not on the amount invested. Check the stated base: \"% of offering price\" and \"% of amount invested\" give different answers.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Load in ringgit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{load} = \\text{amount paid} \\times L$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Shares bought</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{shares} = \\dfrac{\\text{amount paid}\\,(1 - L)}{NAV}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Offer price (load as % of offer price)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P_{\\text{offer}} = \\dfrac{NAV}{1 - L} = \\dfrac{10}{0.97} = \\text{RM}10.309$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Same load as a % of NAV</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{L}{1 - L} = \\dfrac{0.03}{0.97} = 3.09\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Return on the outlay after the load</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_{\\text{investor}} = (1 - L)(1 + r) - 1$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Load quoted as % of amount invested</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{amount invested} = \\dfrac{\\text{amount paid}}{1 + L}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l12-fees\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-38\" data-ff-ch=\"c2\" data-ff-text=\"return on equity with margin  v_0, v_1  = value at purchase and at sale,  e_0  = your own money at the start. a margin trade measured against your own equity, with interest and income. include both income and interest, and divide by initial equity, not by the whole position. without costs change in equity leverage scales volatility\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">38</span><h3>Return on equity with margin</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c2\">Chapter 2</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Return} = \\dfrac{\\text{income} - \\text{interest} + V_1 - V_0}{E_0}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$V_0, V_1$ = value at purchase and at sale, $E_0$ = your own money at the start.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> A margin trade measured against your own equity, with interest and income.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{100 - 125 + 7{,}500 - 5{,}000}{2{,}500} = 99\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Include both income and interest, and divide by INITIAL equity, not by the whole position.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Without costs</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_E = \\dfrac{\\Delta V}{E_0} = \\dfrac{R_{\\text{asset}}}{m_{\\text{initial}}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Change in equity</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{E_1 - E_0}{E_0}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Leverage scales volatility</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\sigma_E \\approx \\dfrac{\\sigma_{\\text{asset}}}{m_{\\text{initial}}}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l2-margin\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-39\" data-ff-ch=\"c4a\" data-ff-text=\"ordinary annuity  pmt  = equal payment at the end of each period. equal payments at equal intervals: savings plans, sinking funds. the last payment earns no interest. a payment at time 0 makes it an annuity due. present value of an annuity annuity due (payments at the start) payment needed to reach a target loan instalment interest earned\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">39</span><h3>Ordinary annuity</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c4a\">Appendix 4A</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$FV = PMT\\left[\\dfrac{(1+r)^n - 1}{r}\\right]$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$PMT$ = equal payment at the END of each period.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Equal payments at equal intervals: savings plans, sinking funds.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$1{,}000\\left[\\dfrac{1.08^8 - 1}{0.08}\\right] = \\text{RM}10{,}636.63$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>The last payment earns no interest. A payment at time 0 makes it an annuity due.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Present value of an annuity</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$PV = PMT\\left[\\dfrac{1 - (1+r)^{-n}}{r}\\right]$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Annuity due (payments at the start)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$FV_{\\text{due}} = FV_{\\text{ordinary}} \\times (1 + r)$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Payment needed to reach a target</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$PMT = \\dfrac{FV}{\\bigl[(1+r)^n - 1\\bigr]/r}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Loan instalment</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$PMT = \\dfrac{PV}{\\bigl[1 - (1+r)^{-n}\\bigr]/r}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Interest earned</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Interest} = FV - n \\times PMT$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l4-annuity\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-40\" data-ff-ch=\"c4a\" data-ff-text=\"value of a cash-flow stream  cf_t  = cash flow at the end of period  t . unequal cash flows (mixed stream): discount each one separately. the annuity shortcut needs equal payments. never add up cash flows without discounting them. net present value satisfactory investment\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">40</span><h3>Value of a cash-flow stream</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c4a\">Appendix 4A</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$PV = \\sum_{t=1}^{n} \\dfrac{CF_t}{(1+r)^t}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$CF_t$ = cash flow at the end of period $t$.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Unequal cash flows (mixed stream): discount each one separately.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\begin{gathered} CF = 30,\\ 40,\\ 50,\\ 60,\\ 70 \\ \\text{at } 8\\% \\\\ PV = \\dfrac{30}{1.08} + \\dfrac{40}{1.08^2} + \\dfrac{50}{1.08^3} + \\dfrac{60}{1.08^4} + \\dfrac{70}{1.08^5} = 193.51 \\end{gathered}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>The annuity shortcut needs EQUAL payments. Never add up cash flows without discounting them.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Net present value</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$NPV = PV(\\text{benefits}) - PV(\\text{costs})$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Satisfactory investment</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$PV(\\text{benefits}) \\ge PV(\\text{costs})$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l4-annuity\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-41\" data-ff-ch=\"c4\" data-ff-text=\"yield, irr and growth rate the compound annual rate that turns  pv  into  fv  in  n  years. annualise a multi-year return so different horizons can be compared.  40 %  over  5  years is not  40 % / 5 = 8 %  a year. irr of a stream of cash flows irr decision rule growth rate geometric average return\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">41</span><h3>Yield, IRR and growth rate</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c4\">Chapter 4</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$r = \\left(\\dfrac{FV}{PV}\\right)^{1/n} - 1$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">The compound annual rate that turns $PV$ into $FV$ in $n$ years.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Annualise a multi-year return so different horizons can be compared.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\left(\\dfrac{1{,}400}{1{,}000}\\right)^{1/5} - 1 \\approx 6.96\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>$40\\%$ over $5$ years is NOT $40\\% / 5 = 8\\%$ a year.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">IRR of a stream of cash flows</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{n} \\dfrac{CF_t}{(1 + IRR)^t}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">IRR decision rule</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$IRR \\ge R_{\\text{required}} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\text{acceptable}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Growth rate</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$g = \\left(\\dfrac{D_n}{D_0}\\right)^{1/n} - 1$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Geometric average return</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\bar{R}_{\\text{geo}} = \\Bigl[\\prod_{t=1}^{n} (1 + R_t)\\Bigr]^{1/n} - 1$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l4-hpr\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-42\" data-ff-ch=\"c6\" data-ff-text=\"ownership percentage and dilution recompute the denominator after every issue, rights offering or buyback. new shares, rights offerings, buybacks and class structures: has your slice of the company changed? an unchanged number of shares does not mean an unchanged percentage. a lower percentage (dilution) is not automatically a loss of wealth: that depends on the issue price and the use of funds. after new shares sold to others rights offering,  1  new for every  m  held, you subscribe rights offering, everyone else subscribes, you do not rights entitlement and cost buyback from others, you keep your shares voting share with classified stock\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">42</span><h3>Ownership percentage and dilution</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c6\">Chapter 6</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Ownership} = \\dfrac{\\text{shares you hold}}{\\text{shares outstanding}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Recompute the denominator after every issue, rights offering or buyback.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> New shares, rights offerings, buybacks and class structures: has your slice of the company changed?</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{100}{1{,}000} = 10\\% \\;\\to\\; \\dfrac{100}{1{,}000 + 200} = 8.33\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>An unchanged number of shares does not mean an unchanged percentage. A lower percentage (dilution) is not automatically a loss of wealth: that depends on the issue price and the use of funds.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">After new shares sold to others</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Ownership} = \\dfrac{n}{N + \\text{new shares}} = \\dfrac{100}{1{,}100} = 9.09\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Rights offering, $1$ new for every $m$ held, you subscribe</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{n + n/m}{N + N/m} = \\dfrac{12{,}500}{1{,}250{,}000} = 1\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Rights offering, everyone else subscribes, you do not</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{n}{N + (N - n)/m} = \\dfrac{10{,}000}{1{,}247{,}500} = 0.8016\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Rights entitlement and cost</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{new shares} = \\dfrac{n}{m} = 2{,}500;\\quad \\text{cost} = 2{,}500 \\times \\text{RM}4 = \\text{RM}10{,}000$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Buyback from others, you keep your shares</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Ownership} = \\dfrac{n}{N - \\text{shares repurchased}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Voting share with classified stock</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{\\text{your shares} \\times \\text{votes per share}}{\\text{total votes}} = \\dfrac{1{,}000 \\times 10}{10{,}000 + 5{,}000} = 66.7\\%$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l6-actions\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-43\" data-ff-ch=\"c6\" data-ff-text=\"market capitalisation  p  = current share price,  n  = common shares outstanding. the stock-market value of the whole common equity; also the basis of the small-, mid- and large-cap labels. a higher share price does not mean a larger company. market cap is equity value only, not enterprise value: it ignores the debt. price implied by market cap size classes (slides, u.s., 2014)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">43</span><h3>Market capitalisation</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c6\">Chapter 6</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Market cap} = P \\times N$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$P$ = current share price, $N$ = common shares outstanding.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> The stock-market value of the whole common equity; also the basis of the small-, mid- and large-cap labels.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$5 \\times 100\\text{m} = \\text{RM}500\\text{m}\\ \\text{vs}\\ 50 \\times 2\\text{m} = \\text{RM}100\\text{m}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>A higher share price does not mean a larger company. Market cap is equity value only, not enterprise value: it ignores the debt.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Price implied by market cap</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P = \\dfrac{\\text{Market cap}}{N}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Size classes (slides, U.S., 2014)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{small} &lt; \\$2\\text{bn} \\le \\text{mid} \\le \\$10\\text{bn} &lt; \\text{large}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l6-value\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-44\" data-ff-ch=\"c7\" data-ff-text=\"inventory turnover slide definition: sales over year-end inventory. a common alternative matches cost to cost: cogs over average inventory. how quickly stock is sold. slow turnover can mean weak demand or obsolete goods. the two versions can give different answers: use the one stated. higher is not always better, because too little stock loses sales. cost of goods sold over average inventory days of inventory: extension\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">44</span><h3>Inventory turnover</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c7\">Chapter 7</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Inventory turnover} = \\dfrac{\\text{annual sales}}{\\text{inventory}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Slide definition: sales over year-end inventory. A common alternative matches cost to cost: COGS over average inventory.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> How quickly stock is sold. Slow turnover can mean weak demand or obsolete goods.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{12}{3} = 4\\times$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>The two versions can give different answers: use the one stated. Higher is not always better, because too little stock loses sales.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Cost of goods sold over average inventory</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{COGS}{(\\text{Inv}_0 + \\text{Inv}_1)/2} \\qquad \\dfrac{8}{2} = 4\\times,\\quad \\dfrac{180}{(30 + 60)/2} = 4\\times$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Days of inventory: extension</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Days} \\approx \\dfrac{365}{\\text{inventory turnover}} \\qquad \\dfrac{365}{4} = 91.25\\ \\text{days}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l7-ratios\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-45\" data-ff-ch=\"c7\" data-ff-text=\"total asset turnover sales generated per ringgit of assets; the second dupont driver. asset efficiency: how hard the whole asset base works to support sales. sales goes on top. a high turnover says nothing about margins or the age of the assets. average total assets (apex)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">45</span><h3>Total asset turnover</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c7\">Chapter 7</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Total asset turnover} = \\dfrac{\\text{annual sales}}{\\text{total assets}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Sales generated per ringgit of assets; the second DuPont driver.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Asset efficiency: how hard the whole asset base works to support sales.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{12}{6} = 2.0\\times$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Sales goes on top. A high turnover says nothing about margins or the age of the assets.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Average total assets (Apex)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{300}{(200 + 240)/2} = \\dfrac{300}{220} = 1.36\\times$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l7-ratios\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-46\" data-ff-ch=\"c7\" data-ff-text=\"debt-equity ratio the slide uses long-term debt; other sources use interest-bearing debt or total liabilities. financial leverage: how much debt supports the business relative to the owners&#x27; money. check the numerator. the practice paper uses total liabilities ( 160 / 80 = 2.0 ), so the same firm can show very different ratios. negative or tiny equity makes the ratio meaningless. total-liabilities version equity as a share of total assets (table 7.7)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">46</span><h3>Debt-equity ratio</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c7\">Chapter 7</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Debt-equity} = \\dfrac{\\text{long-term debt}}{\\text{stockholders' equity}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">The slide uses long-term debt; other sources use interest-bearing debt or total liabilities.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Financial leverage: how much debt supports the business relative to the owners' money.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{3}{4} = 0.75\\times$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Check the numerator. The practice paper uses TOTAL liabilities ($160 / 80 = 2.0$), so the same firm can show very different ratios. Negative or tiny equity makes the ratio meaningless.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Total-liabilities version</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{\\text{total liabilities}}{\\text{equity}} \\qquad \\dfrac{800{,}000}{400{,}000} = 2.0\\times$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Equity as a share of total assets (Table 7.7)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{\\text{equity}}{\\text{total assets}} = \\dfrac{1}{EM} \\qquad \\dfrac{294.5}{941.2} = 31.3\\%$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l7-ratios\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-47\" data-ff-ch=\"c7\" data-ff-text=\"times interest earned  ebit  = earnings before interest and taxes. coverage: how many times operating earnings cover the interest bill. use ebit, not net income or sales. it is an earnings cushion, not a cash-flow guarantee, and it ignores principal repayments. zero interest makes it undefined. stress test (apex) ebit from the income statement (table 7.4)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">47</span><h3>Times interest earned</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c7\">Chapter 7</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Times interest earned} = \\dfrac{EBIT}{\\text{interest expense}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$EBIT$ = earnings before interest and taxes.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Coverage: how many times operating earnings cover the interest bill.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{1.2}{0.3} = 4\\times$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Use EBIT, not net income or sales. It is an earnings cushion, not a cash-flow guarantee, and it ignores principal repayments. Zero interest makes it undefined.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Stress test (Apex)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{40}{10} = 4\\times \\;\\to\\; \\dfrac{20}{10} = 2\\times$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">EBIT from the income statement (Table 7.4)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$EBIT = EBT + \\text{interest} \\qquad 221.8 + 13.4 = 235.2$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l7-ratios\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-48\" data-ff-ch=\"c6 c7\" data-ff-text=\"book value per share and price-to-book book value = assets minus liabilities (minus any preferred claims), at accounting values. how aggressively the market prices the stock relative to its accounting net worth. divide the price by book value per share, not by total equity. a low p/b is not proof of a bargain, and a p/b above 1 is not proof of overpricing. two-step chain (q29, beacon) book equity as a residual book value per common share (ch 6) common book equity (ch 6) from common equity directly (ch 6) price-to-book ratio (ch 6)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">48</span><h3>Book value per share and price-to-book</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c6\">Chapter 6</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c7\">Chapter 7</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$BVPS = \\dfrac{\\text{common shareholders' equity}}{\\text{common shares outstanding}},\\qquad P/B = \\dfrac{\\text{market price per share}}{BVPS}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Book value = assets minus liabilities (minus any preferred claims), at accounting values.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> How aggressively the market prices the stock relative to its accounting net worth.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$BVPS = \\dfrac{4\\text{m}}{1\\text{m}} = \\text{RM}4,\\qquad P/B = \\dfrac{6}{4} = 1.5\\times$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Divide the PRICE by BOOK VALUE PER SHARE, not by total equity. A low P/B is not proof of a bargain, and a P/B above 1 is not proof of overpricing.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Two-step chain (Q29, Beacon)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{10}{8/2} = 2.5\\times,\\qquad \\dfrac{30}{96/12} = 3.75\\times$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Book equity as a residual</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Equity} = \\text{assets} - \\text{liabilities} \\qquad 10 - 6 = 4$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Book value per common share (Ch 6)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$BVPS = \\dfrac{\\text{total assets} - \\text{liabilities} - \\text{preferred stock}}{\\text{common shares outstanding}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Common book equity (Ch 6)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Common equity} = \\text{assets} - \\text{liabilities} - \\text{preferred} = \\text{RM}80\\text{m}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">From common equity directly (Ch 6)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$BVPS = \\dfrac{\\text{common equity}}{\\text{common shares}} = \\dfrac{\\text{RM}12\\text{m}}{3\\text{m}} = \\text{RM}4$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Price-to-book ratio (Ch 6)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{P}{BVPS} = \\dfrac{6}{4} = 1.5$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l7-pershare\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-49\" data-ff-ch=\"c8\" data-ff-text=\"expected return against the required return  p_0  = price paid today,  d_1  and  p_1  = forecast dividend and price one year ahead,  k  = required return. the second way to make the decision: is the forecast one-year return above the hurdle?  e(r) &gt; k  is exactly the same test as  v_0 &gt; p_0 ; the two methods must agree. an expected return is a forecast, not a promise. equivalence of the two tests dividend yield plus capital gain\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">49</span><h3>Expected return against the required return</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c8\">Chapter 8</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$E(R) = \\dfrac{D_1 + (P_1 - P_0)}{P_0} \\quad\\text{vs}\\quad k$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$P_0$ = price paid today, $D_1$ and $P_1$ = forecast dividend and price one year ahead, $k$ = required return.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> The second way to make the decision: is the forecast one-year return above the hurdle?</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{1.20 + 24 - 22}{22} = 14.55\\% &gt; 12\\%;\\qquad \\dfrac{2 + 42 - 40}{40} = 10\\% &lt; 11\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>$E(R) &gt; k$ is exactly the same test as $V_0 &gt; P_0$; the two methods must agree. An expected return is a forecast, not a promise.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Equivalence of the two tests</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$E(R) &gt; k \\iff \\dfrac{D_1 + P_1}{1+k} &gt; P_0$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Dividend yield plus capital gain</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$E(R) = \\dfrac{D_1}{P_0} + \\dfrac{P_1 - P_0}{P_0}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l8-required\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-50\" data-ff-ch=\"c9\" data-ff-text=\"on-balance volume (obv)  v_t  = shares traded on day  t ,  p_t  = closing price. the starting level is arbitrary. track whether volume is heavier on up days or down days; its direction is read, not its level. add or subtract the whole day&#x27;s volume according to the direction of the close; the size of the price change is irrelevant, and an unchanged close adds nothing. several days at once\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">50</span><h3>On-balance volume (OBV)</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c9\">Chapter 9</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$OBV_t = OBV_{t-1} + \\begin{cases} +V_t &amp; \\text{if } P_t &gt; P_{t-1} \\\\ -V_t &amp; \\text{if } P_t &lt; P_{t-1} \\\\ 0 &amp; \\text{if } P_t = P_{t-1} \\end{cases}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$V_t$ = shares traded on day $t$, $P_t$ = closing price. The starting level is arbitrary.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Track whether volume is heavier on up days or down days; its direction is read, not its level.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$10 + 3 - 2 = 11 \\text{ million shares}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Add or subtract the WHOLE day's volume according to the direction of the close; the size of the price change is irrelevant, and an unchanged close adds nothing.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Several days at once</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$OBV_T = OBV_0 + \\sum_{\\text{up days}} V_t - \\sum_{\\text{down days}} V_t$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l9-charts\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-51\" data-ff-ch=\"c9\" data-ff-text=\"mutual fund cash ratio (mfcr) cash and total assets of the same group of funds, on the same date. gauge funds&#x27; buying power. the slides read a high ratio as bullish (cash waiting to be invested).  12/200 = 6 % , not  0.6 %  or  16.67 % . a high ratio may also be cash held for redemptions or caution, so it is not a reliable signal on its own. cash implied by a ratio\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">51</span><h3>Mutual fund cash ratio (MFCR)</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c9\">Chapter 9</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{MFCR} = \\dfrac{\\text{mutual fund cash}}{\\text{assets under management}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Cash and total assets of the same group of funds, on the same date.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Gauge funds' buying power. The slides read a high ratio as bullish (cash waiting to be invested).</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{\\text{RM}12\\text{m}}{\\text{RM}200\\text{m}} = 6\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>$12/200 = 6\\%$, not $0.6\\%$ or $16.67\\%$. A high ratio may also be cash held for redemptions or caution, so it is not a reliable signal on its own.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Cash implied by a ratio</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{cash} = \\text{MFCR} \\times \\text{assets under management}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l9-indicators\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-52\" data-ff-ch=\"c9\" data-ff-text=\"abnormal (excess) return  r_  text actual    = observed return,  e(r)  = return an appropriate risk model expects for the same exposure; the difference is in percentage points. before calling any return &quot;abnormal&quot; or any pattern an anomaly: compare with the risk-adjusted benchmark, then subtract costs. positive is not abnormal:  15 %  against an expected  16 %  is  -1  point. a positive estimate may still be model error, missing risk or chance. after trading costs net strategy return (subjective q2) cumulative abnormal return (event studies, figure 9.4) a risk model for e(r): capm from chapter 5\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">52</span><h3>Abnormal (excess) return</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c9\">Chapter 9</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$AR = R_{\\text{actual}} - E(R)$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$R_{\\text{actual}}$ = observed return, $E(R)$ = return an appropriate risk model expects for the same exposure; the difference is in percentage points.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Before calling any return \"abnormal\" or any pattern an anomaly: compare with the risk-adjusted benchmark, then subtract costs.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$12\\% - 10\\% = 2 \\text{ points};\\quad 2 - 1.5 = 0.5 \\text{ point after costs}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Positive is not abnormal: $15\\%$ against an expected $16\\%$ is $-1$ point. A positive estimate may still be model error, missing risk or chance.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">After trading costs</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$AR_{\\text{net}} = R_{\\text{actual}} - E(R) - \\text{costs}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Net strategy return (subjective Q2)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_{\\text{net}} = R_{\\text{gross}} - \\text{costs} = 3.2\\% - 0.5\\% = 2.7\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Cumulative abnormal return (event studies, Figure 9.4)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$CAR_T = \\sum_{t=1}^{T} AR_t$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">A risk model for E(R): CAPM from Chapter 5</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$E(R) = R_f + \\beta\\,\\bigl[E(R_m) - R_f\\bigr]$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l9-efficient\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-53\" data-ff-ch=\"c10\" data-ff-text=\"convertible payback period  c  = annual bond interest,  n  = conversion ratio,  d  = annual dividend per share. how many years of the bond&#x27;s extra income it takes to recover the conversion premium. use the income difference, not the full coupon ( 100 / 60  is wrong). if  c  le n d  there is no ordinary payback. ignores time value and changes. dividends on the conversion shares extra annual income\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">53</span><h3>Convertible payback period</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c10\">Chapter 10</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Payback} = \\dfrac{\\text{conversion premium}}{C - N \\times D}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$C$ = annual bond interest, $N$ = conversion ratio, $D$ = annual dividend per share.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> How many years of the bond's EXTRA income it takes to recover the conversion premium.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{100}{60 - 20 \\times 2} = \\dfrac{100}{20} = 5 \\text{ years}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Use the income difference, not the full coupon ($100 / 60$ is wrong). If $C \\le N D$ there is no ordinary payback. Ignores time value and changes.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Dividends on the conversion shares</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Dividends} = N \\times D = 20 \\times 2 = \\text{RM}40$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Extra annual income</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$C - N D = 60 - 40 = \\text{RM}20$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l10-convertible\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-54\" data-ff-ch=\"c12\" data-ff-text=\"hpr with reinvested distributions  n_0, n_1  = shares held at the start and end;  d  = distribution per share;  p_  text reinvest    = price at which it buys new shares. distributions are reinvested in more fund shares (the slide 12-42 share-count form). never add the reinvested distribution again: it already bought the extra shares inside  n_1 p_1 . new shares bought subjective q4(d)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">54</span><h3>HPR with reinvested distributions</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c12\">Chapter 12</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$HPR = \\dfrac{N_1 P_1 - N_0 P_0}{N_0 P_0},\\qquad N_1 = N_0 + \\dfrac{N_0 D}{P_{\\text{reinvest}}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$N_0, N_1$ = shares held at the start and end; $D$ = distribution per share; $P_{\\text{reinvest}}$ = price at which it buys new shares.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Distributions are reinvested in more fund shares (the slide 12-42 share-count form).</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$N_1 = 100 + \\dfrac{50}{10} = 105;\\quad \\dfrac{105 \\times 10.50 - 1{,}000}{1{,}000} = 10.25\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Never add the reinvested distribution again: it already bought the extra shares inside $N_1 P_1$.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">New shares bought</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\Delta N = \\dfrac{\\text{total distribution}}{P_{\\text{reinvest}}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Subjective Q4(d)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{105 \\times 10.40 - 1{,}000}{1{,}000} = 9.2\\%$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l12-returns\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t1\" id=\"ff-55\" data-ff-ch=\"c8\" data-ff-text=\"zero-growth dividend model  d  = the level annual dividend paid forever, first payment one year from now;  k  = required return. a dividend expected to stay the same forever (a level perpetuity). divide by  k  as a decimal ( 0.10 , not  10 ). it is valid only if the dividend really is level forever, not merely because the last one was   text rm 2 . return implied by the price special case of constant growth\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">55</span><h3>Zero-growth dividend model</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c8\">Chapter 8</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$V_0 = \\dfrac{D}{k}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$D$ = the level annual dividend paid forever, first payment one year from now; $k$ = required return.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> A dividend expected to stay the same forever (a level perpetuity).</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$V_0 = \\dfrac{2}{0.10} = \\text{RM}20$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Divide by $k$ as a decimal ($0.10$, not $10$). It is valid only if the dividend really is level forever, not merely because the last one was $\\text{RM}2$.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Return implied by the price</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$k = \\dfrac{D}{P_0}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Special case of constant growth</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$g = 0 \\;\\Rightarrow\\; \\dfrac{D_1}{k - g} = \\dfrac{D}{k}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l8-ddm\">Practise in the lesson</button></article></div><div class=\"ff-tier\" data-ff-tier=\"2\"><h3 class=\"ff-tier-title\"><span>Tier 2</span> Should know</h3><p class=\"ff-tier-note\">Common in calculation questions or as the second step of a longer one.</p><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-56\" data-ff-ch=\"c2\" data-ff-text=\"short sale  n  = number of shares borrowed and sold. borrow, sell, buy back, return. you win if the price falls. a rising price hurts the short seller, and the loss has no ceiling. the best case is the sale proceeds. payoff limits funds held by the broker short-account margin margin-call price\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">56</span><h3>Short sale</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c2\">Chapter 2</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Profit} = (P_{\\text{sell}} - P_{\\text{buy back}}) \\times N$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$N$ = number of shares borrowed and sold.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Borrow, sell, buy back, return. You win if the price falls.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$(25 - 31) \\times 300 = -\\text{RM}1{,}800$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>A rising price hurts the short seller, and the loss has no ceiling. The best case is the sale proceeds.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Payoff limits</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Max gain} = N \\times P_{\\text{sell}},\\qquad \\text{Max loss} = \\text{unlimited}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Funds held by the broker</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Funds held} = \\text{proceeds} + \\text{margin deposit}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Short-account margin</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Margin} = \\dfrac{\\text{funds held} - N P}{N P}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Margin-call price</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P^{*} = \\dfrac{\\text{funds held}}{N\\,(1 + m)}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l2-short\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-57\" data-ff-ch=\"c4\" data-ff-text=\"standard deviation and cv  r_t  = return in period  t ,   bar r  = average return,  n - 1  for a sample. absolute spread of returns around the mean. use  n - 1  when told it is a sample. a historical   sigma  is an estimate, not a promise. average return coefficient of variation (risk per unit of return)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">57</span><h3>Standard deviation and CV</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c4\">Chapter 4</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$s = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\sum_{t=1}^{n} (R_t - \\bar{R})^2}{n - 1}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$R_t$ = return in period $t$, $\\bar R$ = average return, $n - 1$ for a sample.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Absolute spread of returns around the mean.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$3, 6, 9, 12, 15:\\ \\ s = \\sqrt{\\dfrac{90}{4}} \\approx 4.74\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Use $n - 1$ when told it is a sample. A historical $\\sigma$ is an estimate, not a promise.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Average return</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\bar{R} = \\dfrac{\\sum R_t}{n}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Coefficient of variation (risk per unit of return)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$CV = \\dfrac{\\sigma}{\\bar{r}}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l4-risk\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-58\" data-ff-ch=\"c1 c4\" data-ff-text=\"real return  i  = inflation rate. this is the quick approximation. what your money really earns after prices rise. use the approximation when it is asked for; the exact (fisher) answer is slightly different. exact (fisher equation) purchasing power of a nominal amount after tax and inflation\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">58</span><h3>Real return</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c1\">Chapter 1</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c4\">Chapter 4</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$R_{\\text{real}} \\approx R_{\\text{nominal}} - i$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$i$ = inflation rate. This is the quick approximation.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> What your money really earns after prices rise.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$8\\% - 5\\% \\approx 3\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Use the approximation when it is asked for; the exact (Fisher) answer is slightly different.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Exact (Fisher equation)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_{\\text{real}} = \\dfrac{1 + R_{\\text{nominal}}}{1 + i} - 1$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Purchasing power of a nominal amount</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Real value} = \\dfrac{\\text{nominal value}}{1 + i}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">After tax and inflation</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_{\\text{real}} \\approx R(1 - t) - i$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l4-return\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-59\" data-ff-ch=\"c1\" data-ff-text=\"after-tax return  t  = tax rate. compare investments on what you actually keep. a higher pre-tax return is not automatically better. apply the tax first.\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">59</span><h3>After-tax return</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c1\">Chapter 1</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$R_{\\text{after-tax}} = R_{\\text{pre-tax}}\\,(1 - t)$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$t$ = tax rate.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Compare investments on what you actually keep.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$10\\%(1 - 0.30) = 7.0\\%\\quad\\text{vs}\\quad 8\\%(1 - 0.05) = 7.6\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>A higher pre-tax return is not automatically better. Apply the tax first.</p></div></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l1-life\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-60\" data-ff-ch=\"c2\" data-ff-text=\"bid, ask and spread quotes are from the dealer&#x27;s side: the dealer buys at the bid and sells at the ask. what a dealer trade costs you. if you sell, you receive the bid; if you buy, you pay the ask. &quot;ask&quot; is not what the investor asks. selling and buying round-trip cost percentage trading cost\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">60</span><h3>Bid, ask and spread</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c2\">Chapter 2</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Spread} = \\text{Ask} - \\text{Bid},\\qquad \\text{Bid} &lt; \\text{Ask}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Quotes are from the dealer's side: the dealer buys at the bid and sells at the ask.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> What a dealer trade costs you.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$100.00 - 99.50 = 0.50$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>If you sell, you receive the bid; if you buy, you pay the ask. \"Ask\" is not what the investor asks.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Selling and buying</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Proceeds} = N \\times \\text{Bid},\\qquad \\text{Cost} = N \\times \\text{Ask}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Round-trip cost</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$N\\,(\\text{Ask} - \\text{Bid})$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Percentage trading cost</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Cost} = c \\times \\text{trade value}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l2-trading\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-61\" data-ff-ch=\"c6\" data-ff-text=\"stock split mechanics  k -for- 1  split;  n  = shares held (or outstanding),  p  = price per share; mechanical reference price, before other market moves. any split or reverse split: shares and price move in opposite directions and the value stays the same. moving shares and price in the same direction, or calling the split a gain. ownership percentage and holding value are unchanged by the split itself. value unchanged reverse split,  1 -for- k \"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">61</span><h3>Stock split mechanics</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c6\">Chapter 6</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$N_{\\text{new}} = k \\times N_{\\text{old}},\\qquad P_{\\text{new}} \\approx \\dfrac{P_{\\text{old}}}{k}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$k$-for-$1$ split; $N$ = shares held (or outstanding), $P$ = price per share; mechanical reference price, before other market moves.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Any split or reverse split: shares and price move in opposite directions and the value stays the same.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$120 \\times 3 = 360\\ \\text{shares at}\\ \\dfrac{30}{3} = \\text{RM}10:\\ 360 \\times 10 = \\text{RM}3{,}600$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Moving shares and price in the same direction, or calling the split a gain. Ownership percentage and holding value are unchanged by the split itself.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Value unchanged</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$N_{\\text{new}} P_{\\text{new}} = N_{\\text{old}} P_{\\text{old}} = 200 \\times 100 = 400 \\times 50 = \\text{RM}20{,}000$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Reverse split, $1$-for-$k$</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$N_{\\text{new}} = \\dfrac{N_{\\text{old}}}{k},\\qquad P_{\\text{new}} \\approx k \\times P_{\\text{old}}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l6-actions\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-62\" data-ff-ch=\"c7\" data-ff-text=\"peg ratio growth entered as a whole number of percent:  10 %  is entered as  10 . is the p/e high relative to the expected growth in earnings?  15 / 0.10 = 150  uses the wrong convention. the thresholds around 1 are rules of thumb, and the answer is only as good as the growth forecast. slide rules of thumb implied p/e for a target peg\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">62</span><h3>PEG ratio</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c7\">Chapter 7</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$PEG = \\dfrac{P/E}{\\text{3- to 5-year EPS growth rate, in percent}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Growth entered as a whole number of percent: $10\\%$ is entered as $10$.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Is the P/E high relative to the expected growth in earnings?</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{15}{10} = 1.5$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>$15 / 0.10 = 150$ uses the wrong convention. The thresholds around 1 are rules of thumb, and the answer is only as good as the growth forecast.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Slide rules of thumb</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$PEG &lt; 1:\\ \\text{may be undervalued};\\quad PEG = 1:\\ \\text{in line};\\quad PEG &gt; 1:\\ \\text{may be fully valued}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Implied P/E for a target PEG</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P/E = PEG \\times g \\qquad 1 \\times 12 = 12$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l7-pershare\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-63\" data-ff-ch=\"c8\" data-ff-text=\"relative valuation (price multiples) measures: eps (p/e), cash flow per share (p/cf), sales per share (p/s), book value per share (p/b). the multiple comes from comparable firms or the market. a quick cross-check against peers, or a future price from forecast eps. p/s is usable when earnings are negative. match the date and definition of the measure to the multiple; a year-ahead price must be discounted. a low multiple alone does not prove undervaluation. price from p/e a multiple from market data price from p/cf, p/s or p/b peg ratio (extension, not in the slides)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">63</span><h3>Relative valuation (price multiples)</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c8\">Chapter 8</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Implied price} = \\text{per-share measure} \\times \\text{chosen multiple}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Measures: EPS (P/E), cash flow per share (P/CF), sales per share (P/S), book value per share (P/B). The multiple comes from comparable firms or the market.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> A quick cross-check against peers, or a future price from forecast EPS. P/S is usable when earnings are negative.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$12 \\times 2.50 = 30,\\quad 8 \\times 4.00 = 32,\\quad 1.2 \\times 20 = 24,\\quad 2.5 \\times 10 = 25$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Match the date and definition of the measure to the multiple; a year-ahead price must be discounted. A low multiple alone does not prove undervaluation.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Price from P/E</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P = EPS \\times P/E$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">A multiple from market data</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P/E = \\dfrac{P_0}{EPS},\\quad P/CF = \\dfrac{P_0}{CFPS},\\quad P/S = \\dfrac{P_0}{SPS},\\quad P/B = \\dfrac{P_0}{BVPS}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Price from P/CF, P/S or P/B</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P = CFPS \\times P/CF = SPS \\times P/S = BVPS \\times P/B$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">PEG ratio (extension, not in the slides)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$PEG = \\dfrac{P/E}{\\text{EPS growth in }\\%}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l8-relative\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-64\" data-ff-ch=\"c11\" data-ff-text=\"yield to call (ytc)  n_c  = years to the assumed call date,  cp  = call price (usually par plus a call premium). a callable bond: the yield if the issuer calls on the chosen date. compare it with ytm for each call scenario. use the call price and the call date, not par and maturity. ytc is a scenario, not a prediction that the issuer will call. two-year call example call premium\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">64</span><h3>Yield to call (YTC)</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c11\">Chapter 11</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$P_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{N_c}\\dfrac{C}{(1+YTC)^t} + \\dfrac{CP}{(1+YTC)^{N_c}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$N_c$ = years to the assumed call date, $CP$ = call price (usually par plus a call premium).</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> A callable bond: the yield if the issuer calls on the chosen date. Compare it with YTM for each call scenario.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\begin{gathered} 1{,}204 = \\sum_{t=1}^{5}\\dfrac{105}{(1+YTC)^t} + \\dfrac{1{,}085}{(1+YTC)^5} \\ \\Rightarrow\\; YTC \\approx 7\\% \\end{gathered}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Use the CALL price and the call date, not par and maturity. YTC is a scenario, not a prediction that the issuer will call.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Two-year call example</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$1{,}000 = \\dfrac{100}{1+YTC} + \\dfrac{1{,}150}{(1+YTC)^2} \\;\\Rightarrow\\; YTC \\approx 12.35\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Call premium</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{call premium} = CP - F$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l11-yields\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-65\" data-ff-ch=\"c12\" data-ff-text=\"back-end load (deferred sales charge)  l_b  = back-end load as a fraction of the stated base (here the pre-fee redemption value). a charge is deducted when shares are sold, under the fund&#x27;s terms. selling is not free: the fee comes out of what you receive. use the base the fund states. load rate from the amounts\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">65</span><h3>Back-end load (deferred sales charge)</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c12\">Chapter 12</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{amount received} = \\text{redemption value} \\times (1 - L_b)$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$L_b$ = back-end load as a fraction of the stated base (here the pre-fee redemption value).</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> A charge is deducted when shares are sold, under the fund's terms.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$10{,}000 \\times (1 - 0.02) = \\text{RM}9{,}800$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Selling is not free: the fee comes out of what you receive. Use the base the fund states.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Load rate from the amounts</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$L_b = \\dfrac{\\text{value} - \\text{amount received}}{\\text{value}} = \\dfrac{200}{10{,}000} = 2\\%$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l12-fees\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-66\" data-ff-ch=\"c12\" data-ff-text=\"expense ratio and operating expenses the expense ratio covers management fees, any 12b-1 fee and other operating costs, as a % of average net assets. find the fund-level cost in ringgit, or the per-share deduction from nav. published returns and nav are already net of these expenses: subtracting the expense ratio again double counts. net return (approximation) year-end deduction from nav (subjective q3) components (table 12.1)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">66</span><h3>Expense ratio and operating expenses</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c12\">Chapter 12</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{annual operating expenses} = \\text{expense ratio} \\times \\text{average net assets}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">The expense ratio covers management fees, any 12b-1 fee and other operating costs, as a % of average net assets.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Find the fund-level cost in ringgit, or the per-share deduction from NAV.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$1\\% \\times \\text{RM}100\\text{m} = \\text{RM}1\\text{m a year}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Published returns and NAV are already net of these expenses: subtracting the expense ratio again double counts.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Net return (approximation)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$r_{\\text{net}} \\approx r_{\\text{gross}} - \\text{expense ratio}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Year-end deduction from NAV (subjective Q3)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$NAV_{\\text{net}} = NAV_{\\text{gross}}\\,(1 - ER) = 11 \\times 0.99 = \\text{RM}10.89$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Components (Table 12.1)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$ER = \\text{management fee} + \\text{12b-1 fee} + \\text{other} = 0.45\\% + 0 + 0.20\\% = 0.65\\%$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l12-fees\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-67\" data-ff-ch=\"c12\" data-ff-text=\"after-cost ending value (fee drag)  l  = front-end load,  r_  text net    = annual return after the expense ratio,  n  = years. compare what two funds leave you with after loads and ongoing expenses over a horizon. a one-off load and a yearly expense ratio behave differently: the expense ratio compounds, so its cost grows with the horizon. gap between two funds with a back-end load instead\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">67</span><h3>After-cost ending value (fee drag)</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c12\">Chapter 12</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\" title=\"The notes use this in worked examples without stating it as a formula\">implicit</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$FV = \\text{amount paid}\\,(1 - L)\\,(1 + r_{\\text{net}})^{n}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$L$ = front-end load, $r_{\\text{net}}$ = annual return after the expense ratio, $n$ = years.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Compare what two funds leave you with after loads and ongoing expenses over a horizon.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$10{,}000(1.06)^{10} = \\text{RM}17{,}908 \\ \\text{vs}\\ 10{,}000(1.05)^{10} = \\text{RM}16{,}289$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>A one-off load and a yearly expense ratio behave differently: the expense ratio compounds, so its cost grows with the horizon.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Gap between two funds</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{gap} = FV_A - FV_B = 17{,}908 - 16{,}289 = \\text{RM}1{,}619$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">With a back-end load instead</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$FV = \\text{amount}\\,(1 + r_{\\text{net}})^{n}\\,(1 - L_b)$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l12-fees\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-68\" data-ff-ch=\"c3 c6\" data-ff-text=\"net trading profit and costs  c  = commission on each side of the trade. what a trade really made once commissions are paid. trading costs can wipe out an apparent gain. net return on total outlay break-even price rise many trades advisory fee return after transaction costs (ch 6) net gain in ringgit (ch 6)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">68</span><h3>Net trading profit and costs</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c3\">Chapter 3</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c6\">Chapter 6</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Net profit} = N\\,(P_1 - P_0) - (C_{\\text{buy}} + C_{\\text{sell}})$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$C$ = commission on each side of the trade.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> What a trade really made once commissions are paid.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$1{,}000(0.30) - 60 = \\text{RM}240$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Trading costs can wipe out an apparent gain.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Net return on total outlay</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$R_{\\text{net}} = \\dfrac{\\text{net profit}}{N P_0 + C_{\\text{buy}}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Break-even price rise</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\Delta P = \\dfrac{C_{\\text{buy}} + C_{\\text{sell}}}{N}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Many trades</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$k\\,(C_{\\text{buy}} + C_{\\text{sell}})$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Advisory fee</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Fee} = \\text{AUM} \\times f,\\qquad R_{\\text{net}} = R_{\\text{gross}} - f$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Return after transaction costs (Ch 6)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$HPR_{\\text{net}} = \\dfrac{(N P_1 - c_{\\text{sell}}) - (N P_0 + c_{\\text{buy}})}{N P_0 + c_{\\text{buy}}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Net gain in ringgit (Ch 6)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Net gain} = (N P_1 - c_{\\text{sell}}) - (N P_0 + c_{\\text{buy}}) = 622 - 608 = \\text{RM}14$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l3-protect\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-69\" data-ff-ch=\"c3\" data-ff-text=\"order execution rules  p  = market price,  l  = limit price,  s  = stop price. decide whether an order will execute, and at what price. a stop price is a trigger, not a guaranteed price: it becomes a market order when hit. stop triggers loss after a gap fill-or-kill\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">69</span><h3>Order execution rules</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c3\">Chapter 3</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\" title=\"The notes use this in worked examples without stating it as a formula\">implicit</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Buy limit fills if } P \\le L,\\qquad \\text{Sell limit fills if } P \\ge L$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$P$ = market price, $L$ = limit price, $S$ = stop price.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Decide whether an order will execute, and at what price.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\begin{gathered} \\text{Bought at } \\text{RM}20,\\ \\text{stop at } \\text{RM}18,\\ \\text{price gaps to } \\text{RM}14 \\\\ \\text{Loss} = 1{,}000\\,(20 - 14) = \\text{RM}6{,}000 \\end{gathered}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>A stop price is a trigger, not a guaranteed price: it becomes a market order when hit.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Stop triggers</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Sell stop: } P \\le S,\\qquad \\text{Buy stop: } P \\ge S$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Loss after a gap</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Loss} = N\\,(P_0 - P_{\\text{exec}})$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Fill-or-kill</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Execute only if } Q_{\\text{available}} \\ge Q_{\\text{order}}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l3-orders\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-70\" data-ff-ch=\"c3 c12\" data-ff-text=\"relative performance in percentage points. a return means nothing without a matching benchmark. the benchmark must match the portfolio, or the comparison is unreliable. return relative to a benchmark (ch 12)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">70</span><h3>Relative performance</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c3\">Chapter 3</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c12\">Chapter 12</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Relative performance} = R_{\\text{portfolio}} - R_{\\text{benchmark}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">In percentage points.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> A return means nothing without a matching benchmark.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$8\\% - 20\\% = -12\\ \\text{percentage points}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>The benchmark must match the portfolio, or the comparison is unreliable.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Return relative to a benchmark (Ch 12)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{relative return} = R_{\\text{fund}} - R_{\\text{benchmark}}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l3-indexes\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-71\" data-ff-ch=\"c6\" data-ff-text=\"dividend reinvestment and stock dividends  n  = shares held,  d  = cash dividend per share,  p_  text reinvest    = price at which the plan buys (before fees). a drip turns a cash dividend into more shares; a stock dividend pays the shares directly. treating reinvestment as an extra dividend (it uses cash you could have withdrawn), or treating extra shares from a stock dividend as extra wealth. holding after reinvestment stock dividend at rate  s  price after a stock dividend (value unchanged)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">71</span><h3>Dividend reinvestment and stock dividends</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c6\">Chapter 6</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Shares bought by a DRIP} = \\dfrac{n \\times D}{P_{\\text{reinvest}}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$n$ = shares held, $D$ = cash dividend per share, $P_{\\text{reinvest}}$ = price at which the plan buys (before fees).</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> A DRIP turns a cash dividend into more shares; a stock dividend pays the shares directly.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{500 \\times 0.80}{16} = \\dfrac{400}{16} = 25 \\Rightarrow 525\\ \\text{shares}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Treating reinvestment as an extra dividend (it uses cash you could have withdrawn), or treating extra shares from a stock dividend as extra wealth.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Holding after reinvestment</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$n_{\\text{new}} = n + \\dfrac{nD}{P} = 100 + \\dfrac{80}{20} = 104$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Stock dividend at rate $s$</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$n_{\\text{new}} = n(1 + s) = 100 \\times 1.10 = 110$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Price after a stock dividend (value unchanged)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$P_{\\text{new}} \\approx \\dfrac{P}{1 + s} = \\dfrac{22}{1.10} = \\text{RM}20$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l6-dividends\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-72\" data-ff-ch=\"c7\" data-ff-text=\"accounting equation and net income balance sheet: a snapshot at a date. income statement: a flow over a period. check that a balance sheet balances, or find the missing item. book equity is an accounting residual, not what the assets would fetch if sold tomorrow. revenue is not necessarily cash received. equity as the residual net income retained-earnings link: extension\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">72</span><h3>Accounting equation and net income</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c7\">Chapter 7</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Assets} = \\text{Liabilities} + \\text{Equity}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Balance sheet: a snapshot at a date. Income statement: a flow over a period.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Check that a balance sheet balances, or find the missing item.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$10 = 6 + 4\\ \\ (\\text{RM}\\text{ million});\\qquad \\text{Apex: } 240 = 160 + 80$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Book equity is an accounting residual, not what the assets would fetch if sold tomorrow. Revenue is not necessarily cash received.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Equity as the residual</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Equity} = \\text{assets} - \\text{liabilities} = 10 - 6 = 4$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Net income</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Net income} = \\text{revenue} - \\text{expenses} = 10 - 9 = 1$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Retained-earnings link: extension</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$RE_1 = RE_0 + NI - \\text{dividends}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l7-statements\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-73\" data-ff-ch=\"c7\" data-ff-text=\"growth compared with sales growth  x  = any line item: sales, receivables, inventory, debt. spot items growing faster than sales: the slide 7-54 warning signs. a warning sign is not proof. planned expansion, seasonality or new credit terms can explain fast-growing working capital; check before concluding distress or fraud. integrated case cash conversion\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">73</span><h3>Growth compared with sales growth</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c7\">Chapter 7</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\" title=\"The notes use this in worked examples without stating it as a formula\">implicit</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$g = \\dfrac{X_1 - X_0}{X_0}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$X$ = any line item: sales, receivables, inventory, debt.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Spot items growing faster than sales: the slide 7-54 warning signs.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$g_{AR} = \\dfrac{50 - 30}{30} = 66.67\\%,\\quad g_{Inv} = \\dfrac{60 - 30}{30} = 100\\%\\quad\\text{vs sales } 5\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>A warning sign is not proof. Planned expansion, seasonality or new credit terms can explain fast-growing working capital; check before concluding distress or fraud.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Integrated case</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{sales } \\dfrac{12 - 10}{10} = 20\\% \\quad\\text{vs}\\quad \\text{receivables } \\dfrac{2 - 1}{1} = 100\\%$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Cash conversion</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{CFO}{NI} = \\dfrac{0.4}{1.2} = 33\\%$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l7-case\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-74\" data-ff-ch=\"c9\" data-ff-text=\"earnings surprise compare the reported figure (for example eps) with what investors expected before the release. explain the direction of a price reaction to an announcement. compare with expectations, not with last year: earnings that rise to   text rm 1.05  still disappoint a market expecting   text rm 1.20 . percentage surprise\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">74</span><h3>Earnings surprise</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c9\">Chapter 9</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\" title=\"The notes use this in worked examples without stating it as a formula\">implicit</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Surprise} = \\text{actual} - \\text{expected}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Compare the reported figure (for example EPS) with what investors expected before the release.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Explain the direction of a price reaction to an announcement.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$1.20 - 1.00 = +\\text{RM}0.20;\\quad 1.05 - 1.20 = -\\text{RM}0.15$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Compare with expectations, not with last year: earnings that rise to $\\text{RM}1.05$ still disappoint a market expecting $\\text{RM}1.20$.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Percentage surprise</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{\\text{actual} - \\text{expected}}{|\\text{expected}|}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l9-efficient\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-75\" data-ff-ch=\"c10\" data-ff-text=\"call price call premium = the amount added to par when the issuer redeems the bond early. what a bondholder receives if the issuer calls the bond. add the premium to par, never subtract it. the premium rarely offsets the lost above-market coupons. call premium call premium as a percentage of par\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">75</span><h3>Call price</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c10\">Chapter 10</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Call price} = \\text{par} + \\text{call premium}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Call premium = the amount added to par when the issuer redeems the bond early.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> What a bondholder receives if the issuer calls the bond.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$1{,}000 + 30 = \\text{RM}1{,}030$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Add the premium to par, never subtract it. The premium rarely offsets the lost above-market coupons.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Call premium</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Call premium} = \\text{call price} - \\text{par} = 1{,}030 - 1{,}000 = \\text{RM}30$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Call premium as a percentage of par</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{30}{1{,}000} = 3\\%$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l10-terms\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-76\" data-ff-ch=\"c10\" data-ff-text=\"zero-coupon bond  f  = par paid at maturity,  r  = annual required yield,  n  = years to maturity. price or annual return of a bond whose only cash flow is par at maturity.   text rm 200  on   text rm 800  is  25 %  in total, not per year; the annual rate depends on  n . annual return from price and par ringgit gain if held to maturity\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">76</span><h3>Zero-coupon bond</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c10\">Chapter 10</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\" title=\"The notes use this in worked examples without stating it as a formula\">implicit</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$P_0 = \\dfrac{F}{(1 + r)^n}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$F$ = par paid at maturity, $r$ = annual required yield, $n$ = years to maturity.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Price or annual return of a bond whose only cash flow is par at maturity.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$r = \\left(\\dfrac{1{,}000}{800}\\right)^{1/5} - 1 = 4.56\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>$\\text{RM}200$ on $\\text{RM}800$ is $25\\%$ in total, not per year; the annual rate depends on $n$.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Annual return from price and par</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$r = \\left(\\dfrac{F}{P_0}\\right)^{1/n} - 1$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Ringgit gain if held to maturity</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$F - P_0 = 1{,}000 - 800 = \\text{RM}200$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l10-special\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-77\" data-ff-ch=\"c10 c11\" data-ff-text=\"yield spread and basis points compare bonds with matching maturity (and, ideally, currency, tax and options);  1  percentage point  = 100  basis points. measure how much more one bond yields than a comparable benchmark, usually a government bond. a spread bundles credit, liquidity, call, tax and maturity differences. it is not a default probability and not free extra return. basis points yield change as a decimal yield spread in basis points (ch 10) basis point (ch 10)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">77</span><h3>Yield spread and basis points</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c10\">Chapter 10</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c11\">Chapter 11</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Spread} = y_{\\text{bond}} - y_{\\text{benchmark}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Compare bonds with matching maturity (and, ideally, currency, tax and options); $1$ percentage point $= 100$ basis points.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Measure how much more one bond yields than a comparable benchmark, usually a government bond.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$5.5\\% - 4.0\\% = 1.5\\ \\text{percentage points} = 150\\ \\text{bp}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>A spread bundles credit, liquidity, call, tax and maturity differences. It is not a default probability and not free extra return.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Basis points</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$1\\ \\text{bp} = 0.01\\% = 0.0001$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Yield change as a decimal</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$0.20\\ \\text{percentage point} = 20\\ \\text{bp} = 0.002$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Yield spread in basis points (Ch 10)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Spread (bp)} = (y_{\\text{bond}} - y_{\\text{benchmark}}) \\times 10{,}000$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Basis point (Ch 10)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$1 \\text{ bp} = 0.01\\% = 0.0001$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l11-rates\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-78\" data-ff-ch=\"c2\" data-ff-text=\"long purchase cash purchase, no borrowing. the unlevered case, the benchmark for margin and short sales. long losses stop at zero; short losses do not.\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">78</span><h3>Long purchase</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c2\">Chapter 2</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Profit} = N\\,(P_1 - P_0) + \\text{dividends},\\qquad R = \\dfrac{P_1 - P_0}{P_0}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Cash purchase, no borrowing.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> The unlevered case, the benchmark for margin and short sales.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{14 - 10}{10} = 40\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Long losses stop at zero; short losses do not.</p></div></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l2-short\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-79\" data-ff-ch=\"c6\" data-ff-text=\"decline from a market peak measured from the previous peak of a major index. labelling a fall: routine decline   ge 5 % , correction   ge 10 % , bear market   ge 20 %  (the slides&#x27; conventions). dividing by the lower, current level (  dfrac 220  780  = 28.2 % ). one stock falling  20 %  is not a market-wide bear market, and the labels forecast nothing. thresholds (slides) gain needed to recover (extension)\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">79</span><h3>Decline from a market peak</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c6\">Chapter 6</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Decline} = \\dfrac{\\text{peak} - \\text{current level}}{\\text{peak}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Measured from the previous peak of a major index.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Labelling a fall: routine decline $\\ge 5\\%$, correction $\\ge 10\\%$, bear market $\\ge 20\\%$ (the slides' conventions).</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{1{,}000 - 780}{1{,}000} = 22\\% \\Rightarrow \\text{bear market}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Dividing by the lower, current level ($\\dfrac{220}{780} = 28.2\\%$). One stock falling $20\\%$ is not a market-wide bear market, and the labels forecast nothing.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Thresholds (slides)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$5\\%\\ \\text{routine decline},\\quad 10\\%\\ \\text{correction},\\quad 20\\%\\ \\text{bear market}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Gain needed to recover (extension)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{gain needed} = \\dfrac{\\text{peak}}{\\text{current}} - 1 = \\dfrac{1{,}000}{780} - 1 = 28.2\\%$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l6-own\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-80\" data-ff-ch=\"c8\" data-ff-text=\"general dividend valuation model  e(d_t)  = dividend expected at the end of year  t ,  k  = required return. the idea behind every dividend model: zero, constant and variable growth are special cases. a firm paying no dividend today can still have value: later distributions count. a sale price stands in for the dividends after the sale, so do not add both. growth factor per year in present value\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">80</span><h3>General dividend valuation model</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c8\">Chapter 8</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$V_0 = \\sum_{t=1}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{E(D_t)}{(1+k)^t}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$E(D_t)$ = dividend expected at the end of year $t$, $k$ = required return.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> The idea behind every dividend model: zero, constant and variable growth are special cases.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{2}{1.10} + \\dfrac{2}{1.10^2} + \\cdots = \\dfrac{2}{0.10} = \\text{RM}20$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>A firm paying no dividend today can still have value: later distributions count. A sale price stands in for the dividends after the sale, so do not add both.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Growth factor per year in present value</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{PV(D_{t+1})}{PV(D_t)} = \\dfrac{1+g}{1+k} &lt; 1 \\iff k &gt; g$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l8-ddm\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-81\" data-ff-ch=\"c9\" data-ff-text=\"new highs minus new lows  nh_t  = stocks at a  52 -week high on day  t ,  nl_t  = stocks at a  52 -week low. a second breadth gauge; the chapter smooths it with a  10 -day moving average. more highs than lows suggests broad participation, not a guaranteed rise; the count depends on which stocks are covered. ten-day moving average of the difference\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">81</span><h3>New highs minus new lows</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c9\">Chapter 9</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$NH_t - NL_t$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$NH_t$ = stocks at a $52$-week high on day $t$, $NL_t$ = stocks at a $52$-week low.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> A second breadth gauge; the chapter smooths it with a $10$-day moving average.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$90 - 30 = +60$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>More highs than lows suggests broad participation, not a guaranteed rise; the count depends on which stocks are covered.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Ten-day moving average of the difference</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\overline{NH - NL}_{t} = \\dfrac{1}{10}\\sum_{k=0}^{9} (NH_{t-k} - NL_{t-k})$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l9-indicators\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t2\" id=\"ff-82\" data-ff-ch=\"c11\" data-ff-text=\"immunization (duration matching)  h  = the investment horizon (the date of a single known liability). portfolio duration is the value-weighted average of the bonds&#x27; durations. fund a dated liability so that, for small parallel rate changes, the price effect and the reinvestment effect roughly offset. it is a first-order approximation, not a guarantee: large or non-parallel shifts, default, calls and costs can break it, and duration drifts, so rebalance. target (required) wealth horizon wealth = reinvested coupons + sale price which risk dominates\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">82</span><h3>Immunization (duration matching)</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c11\">Chapter 11</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$PV_{\\text{assets}} = PV_{\\text{liability}},\\qquad D_{\\text{assets}} = D_{\\text{liability}}\\ (= H \\text{ for one payment at } H)$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$H$ = the investment horizon (the date of a single known liability). Portfolio duration is the value-weighted average of the bonds' durations.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Fund a dated liability so that, for small parallel rate changes, the price effect and the reinvestment effect roughly offset.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\begin{gathered} 813.67 + 1{,}036.64 = \\text{RM}1{,}850.31 \\ \\approx 1{,}000 \\times 1.08^8 = \\text{RM}1{,}850.93 \\end{gathered}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>It is a first-order approximation, not a guarantee: large or non-parallel shifts, default, calls and costs can break it, and duration drifts, so rebalance.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Target (required) wealth</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$W^{*} = P_0\\,(1+y)^H$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Horizon wealth = reinvested coupons + sale price</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$W_H = \\sum_{t \\le H} CF_t\\,(1+y)^{H-t} + \\sum_{t &gt; H}\\dfrac{CF_t}{(1+y)^{t-H}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Which risk dominates</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$D &gt; H:\\ \\text{price risk};\\qquad D &lt; H:\\ \\text{reinvestment risk}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l11-strategy\">Practise in the lesson</button></article></div><div class=\"ff-tier\" data-ff-tier=\"3\"><h3 class=\"ff-tier-title\"><span>Tier 3</span> Good to know</h3><p class=\"ff-tier-note\">Occasional, mostly conceptual, or used inside the formulas above.</p><article class=\"ff-card ff-t3\" id=\"ff-83\" data-ff-ch=\"c3\" data-ff-text=\"index construction  q  = number of shares outstanding. why different indexes report different &quot;market&quot; returns. a price-weighted index is dominated by the highest-priced stock; a value-weighted one by the largest company. average price value-weighted change equal-weighted change\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">83</span><h3>Index construction</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c3\">Chapter 3</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\" title=\"The notes use this in worked examples without stating it as a formula\">implicit</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Price-weighted change} = \\dfrac{\\sum P_1}{\\sum P_0} - 1$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$Q$ = number of shares outstanding.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Why different indexes report different \"market\" returns.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{10 + 20 + 99}{10 + 20 + 90} - 1 = 7.5\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>A price-weighted index is dominated by the highest-priced stock; a value-weighted one by the largest company.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Average price</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\bar{P} = \\dfrac{\\sum P}{n}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Value-weighted change</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{\\sum P_1 Q}{\\sum P_0 Q} - 1$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Equal-weighted change</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\dfrac{1}{n}\\sum R_i$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l3-indexes\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t3\" id=\"ff-84\" data-ff-ch=\"c2\" data-ff-text=\"underwriting spread the bank buys at  p_  text paid    and sells to the public at  p_  text offer   . how the investment bank is paid, and who carries a shortfall. the underwriter bears the resale risk, not the issuer: the issuer already has its money. issuer proceeds bank profit or loss\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">84</span><h3>Underwriting spread</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c2\">Chapter 2</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Spread} = P_{\\text{offer}} - P_{\\text{paid to issuer}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">The bank buys at $P_{\\text{paid}}$ and sells to the public at $P_{\\text{offer}}$.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> How the investment bank is paid, and who carries a shortfall.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$20 - 19 = 1\\ \\text{per share}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>The underwriter bears the resale risk, not the issuer: the issuer already has its money.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Issuer proceeds</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$N \\times P_{\\text{paid}}$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Bank profit or loss</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$N\\,(P_{\\text{resale}} - P_{\\text{paid}})$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l2-ipo\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t3\" id=\"ff-85\" data-ff-ch=\"c6\" data-ff-text=\"residual claim in a liquidation senior claims: creditors and other claims ranking ahead of common equity, including preferred stock. explaining why common shareholders are residual owners: paid last, with unlimited upside and a downside capped by limited liability. assuming shareholders get their purchase price back. if nothing remains after senior claims, the residual payout is zero (simplified illustration, not a full bankruptcy waterfall). per share\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">85</span><h3>Residual claim in a liquidation</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c6\">Chapter 6</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Residual for common owners} = \\max(0,\\ \\text{amount available} - \\text{senior claims})$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Senior claims: creditors and other claims ranking ahead of common equity, including preferred stock.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Explaining why common shareholders are residual owners: paid last, with unlimited upside and a downside capped by limited liability.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{\\text{RM}1{,}000{,}000}{100{,}000\\ \\text{shares}} = \\text{RM}10\\ \\text{a share}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Assuming shareholders get their purchase price back. If nothing remains after senior claims, the residual payout is zero (simplified illustration, not a full bankruptcy waterfall).</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Per share</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Residual per share} = \\dfrac{\\text{residual}}{\\text{common shares}}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l6-own\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t3\" id=\"ff-86\" data-ff-ch=\"c7\" data-ff-text=\"cash-flow statement identities  cfo ,  cfi ,  cff  = net cash from operating, investing and financing activities. reconcile opening and closing cash; see whether cash came from operations or from borrowing. profit is not cash: a credit sale raises revenue and receivables but not cash until the customer pays. operating cash flow, indirect method (table 7.5) closing cash\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">86</span><h3>Cash-flow statement identities</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c7\">Chapter 7</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\" title=\"The notes use this in worked examples without stating it as a formula\">implicit</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\Delta\\text{Cash} = CFO + CFI + CFF$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$CFO$, $CFI$, $CFF$ = net cash from operating, investing and financing activities.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Reconcile opening and closing cash; see whether cash came from operations or from borrowing.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$202.1 - 150.9 - 35.4 = 15.8;\\qquad 80.0 + 15.8 = 95.8$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Profit is not cash: a credit sale raises revenue and receivables but not cash until the customer pays.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Operating cash flow, indirect method (Table 7.5)</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$CFO = NI + \\text{noncash charges} - \\Delta CA + \\Delta CL \\qquad 139.7 + 77.1 + 5.2 - 41.7 + 21.8 = 202.1$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Closing cash</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Cash}_1 = \\text{Cash}_0 + \\Delta\\text{Cash}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l7-statements\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t3\" id=\"ff-87\" data-ff-ch=\"c7\" data-ff-text=\"intrinsic value versus market price  v  = the analyst&#x27;s estimate of intrinsic value;  p  = market price. express how far the price sits below (or above) an estimate of value.  v  is an estimate built on forecasts, a discount rate and risk, not a guaranteed future price. another analyst can reasonably reach a different  v . buy rule (slide 7-5) earnings surprise\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">87</span><h3>Intrinsic value versus market price</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c7\">Chapter 7</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\" title=\"The notes use this in worked examples without stating it as a formula\">implicit</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Upside to the estimate} = \\dfrac{V - P}{P}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$V$ = the analyst's estimate of intrinsic value; $P$ = market price.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Express how far the price sits below (or above) an estimate of value.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{15 - 12}{12} = 25\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>$V$ is an estimate built on forecasts, a discount rate and risk, not a guaranteed future price. Another analyst can reasonably reach a different $V$.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Buy rule (slide 7-5)</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Buy only if } P \\le V \\qquad P = 10 \\le V = 12$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Earnings surprise</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Surprise} = \\text{actual} - \\text{expected} = 10\\% - 20\\% = -10\\ \\text{points}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l7-topdown\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t3\" id=\"ff-88\" data-ff-ch=\"c9\" data-ff-text=\"confidence index both yields on the same date. high-grade yields are lower, so the index is normally below  1 . a sentiment proxy from the bond market: a narrowing quality spread (ci rising toward  1 ) is read as more risk appetite. keep high-grade on top. interest-rate expectations, liquidity and credit conditions also move it, so it is not a stock-market signal on its own. quality spread\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">88</span><h3>Confidence index</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c9\">Chapter 9</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$CI = \\dfrac{\\text{yield on high-grade corporate bonds}}{\\text{yield on intermediate-grade corporate bonds}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Both yields on the same date. High-grade yields are lower, so the index is normally below $1$.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> A sentiment proxy from the bond market: a narrowing quality spread (CI rising toward $1$) is read as more risk appetite.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{5\\%}{6\\%} \\approx 0.833$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Keep high-grade on top. Interest-rate expectations, liquidity and credit conditions also move it, so it is not a stock-market signal on its own.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Quality spread</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{spread} = y_{\\text{intermediate}} - y_{\\text{high}}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l9-indicators\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t3\" id=\"ff-89\" data-ff-ch=\"c12\" data-ff-text=\"exchange trading cost of an etf purchase paid by the investor on each exchange trade, on top of the fund&#x27;s expense ratio. comparing an etf with a no-load mutual fund for someone who buys small amounts often. a lower expense ratio can be outweighed by per-trade costs on small, frequent purchases. annual cost of holding\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">89</span><h3>Exchange trading cost of an ETF purchase</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c12\">Chapter 12</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\" title=\"The notes use this in worked examples without stating it as a formula\">implicit</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{cost per purchase} \\approx \\text{commission} + \\text{spread or premium paid} \\times \\text{amount}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Paid by the investor on each exchange trade, on top of the fund's expense ratio.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Comparing an ETF with a no-load mutual fund for someone who buys small amounts often.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{\\text{RM}5}{\\text{RM}100} = 5\\%\\ \\text{of each RM100 purchase in commission alone}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>A lower expense ratio can be outweighed by per-trade costs on small, frequent purchases.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Annual cost of holding</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{expense cost} \\approx ER \\times \\text{average balance}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l12-choose\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t3\" id=\"ff-90\" data-ff-ch=\"c1\" data-ff-text=\"liquidity discount liquidity = fast conversion to cash with little loss of value. how much you give up to sell in a hurry. liquid does not mean safe, and market liquidity is not price stability.\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">90</span><h3>Liquidity discount</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c1\">Chapter 1</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\" title=\"The notes use this in worked examples without stating it as a formula\">implicit</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Discount} = \\dfrac{\\text{fair value} - \\text{quick-sale price}}{\\text{fair value}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Liquidity = fast conversion to cash with little loss of value.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> How much you give up to sell in a hurry.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{1{,}000{,}000 - 750{,}000}{1{,}000{,}000} = 25\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Liquid does not mean safe, and market liquidity is not price stability.</p></div></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l1-short\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t3\" id=\"ff-91\" data-ff-ch=\"c4 c5\" data-ff-text=\"risk and return identities diversification removes only the unsystematic part. which risk is rewarded in capm: only systematic risk. many holdings do not mean immunity from market risk. efficient frontier dominance return in one line\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">91</span><h3>Risk and return identities</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c4\">Chapter 4</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c5\">Chapter 5</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{Total risk} = \\text{systematic (market)} + \\text{unsystematic (firm-specific)}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">Diversification removes only the unsystematic part.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Which risk is rewarded in CAPM: only systematic risk.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\text{Forty bank stocks: still one bet on banking}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Many holdings do not mean immunity from market risk.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Efficient frontier dominance</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{same } \\sigma,\\ \\text{higher } E(R) \\quad\\text{or}\\quad \\text{same } E(R),\\ \\text{lower } \\sigma$$</div></li><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Return in one line</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{Return} = \\text{income} + \\text{change in value}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l5-beta\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t3\" id=\"ff-92\" data-ff-ch=\"c11\" data-ff-text=\"expectations hypothesis (two-period illustration)  y_2  = two-year yield,  r_1  = one-year rate today,  e[r_2]  = one-year rate expected next year (no term premium). show why expected rate rises produce an upward-sloping curve under the pure expectations view. real curves also contain term premiums and segment-specific supply and demand, so you cannot read a certain forecast off the curve. rough average\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">92</span><h3>Expectations hypothesis (two-period illustration)</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c11\">Chapter 11</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\" title=\"The notes use this in worked examples without stating it as a formula\">implicit</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$(1 + y_2)^2 = (1 + r_1)\\,\\bigl(1 + E[r_2]\\bigr)$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">$y_2$ = two-year yield, $r_1$ = one-year rate today, $E[r_2]$ = one-year rate expected next year (no term premium).</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Show why expected rate rises produce an upward-sloping curve under the pure expectations view.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$y_2 = \\sqrt{1.03 \\times 1.05} - 1 \\approx 4.0\\%$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>Real curves also contain term premiums and segment-specific supply and demand, so you cannot read a certain forecast off the curve.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Rough average</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$y_2 \\approx \\dfrac{r_1 + E[r_2]}{2}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l11-curve\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t3\" id=\"ff-93\" data-ff-ch=\"c12\" data-ff-text=\"ownership share of a pooled fund with equal contributions at the same nav, also equal to your contribution over the total pool. how much of the fund&#x27;s net assets your shares represent. you own a share of the fund&#x27;s net assets, not direct title to each underlying security. your claim in ringgit\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">93</span><h3>Ownership share of a pooled fund</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c12\">Chapter 12</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\" title=\"The notes use this in worked examples without stating it as a formula\">implicit</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{ownership share} = \\dfrac{\\text{your shares}}{\\text{shares outstanding}}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">With equal contributions at the same NAV, also equal to your contribution over the total pool.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> How much of the fund's net assets your shares represent.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{1{,}000}{100{,}000} = 1\\%\\ \\text{of net assets}$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>You own a share of the fund's net assets, not direct title to each underlying security.</p></div></div><div class=\"ff-variants\"><h4>Variants and rearrangements <small>lower priority, in order</small></h4><ol><li><span class=\"ff-vname\">Your claim in ringgit</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\">implicit</span><div class=\"ff-vmath\">$$\\text{claim} = \\text{ownership share} \\times \\text{net assets}$$</div></li></ol></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l12-pool\">Practise in the lesson</button></article><article class=\"ff-card ff-t3\" id=\"ff-94\" data-ff-ch=\"c12\" data-ff-text=\"average cost with periodic investment a fixed amount each period buys more shares when the price  p_t  is low. automatic investment plans: what each share cost on average. a lower average cost per share is not a profit and does not protect against a fall in price.\"><header class=\"ff-head\"><span class=\"ff-rank\">94</span><h3>Average cost with periodic investment</h3><span class=\"ff-tags\"><span class=\"ff-tag ff-c12\">Chapter 12</span><span class=\"ff-tag ff-implicit\" title=\"The notes use this in worked examples without stating it as a formula\">implicit</span></span></header><div class=\"ff-base\" data-ff-base>$$\\text{average cost} = \\dfrac{\\text{total invested}}{\\sum_t \\text{amount}_t / P_t}$$</div><p class=\"ff-legend\">A fixed amount each period buys more shares when the price $P_t$ is low.</p><p class=\"ff-when\"><b>Use it when:</b> Automatic investment plans: what each share cost on average.</p><div class=\"ff-grid\"><div class=\"ff-example\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Worked example</span>$$\\dfrac{900}{30 + 25 + 37.5} = \\dfrac{900}{92.5} = \\text{RM}9.73$$</div><div class=\"ff-trap\"><span class=\"ff-label\">Common trap</span><p>A lower average cost per share is not a profit and does not protect against a fall in price.</p></div></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"secondary-btn\" data-nav=\"l12-types\">Practise in the lesson</button></article></div></div>");
